WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.990 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.990 --> 00:00:02.490 align:middle line:90%


00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:03.984 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.984 --> 00:00:04.980 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:18.440 align:middle line:90%


00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:21.830 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: All right,
so last time we

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:24.080 align:middle line:84%
defined the integral of
a non-negative measurable

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:27.800 align:middle line:90%
function, the Lesbesguen rule.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:30.200 align:middle line:84%
Now we are going to
define the Lesbesguen

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:33.660 align:middle line:84%
rule for a general class, a
more general class of functions.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:46.990 align:middle line:84%
So Lesbesgue
integrable functions.

00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:50.660 align:middle line:90%


00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:54.250 align:middle line:90%
So what does this mean?

00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:57.360 align:middle line:90%


00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:07.010 align:middle line:84%
Let E be a measurable
subset of R, a function f,

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:10.670 align:middle line:84%
a measurable function
f, from E to R.

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:30.090 align:middle line:84%
So its real value is
Lesbesgue integrable over E.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:33.720 align:middle line:84%
So I should say this is
a measurable function--

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:42.960 align:middle line:84%
is Lesbesgue integrable
over E if the integral

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:46.200 align:middle line:84%
of the absolute
value of f is finite.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:52.650 align:middle line:90%


00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:58.090 align:middle line:90%
So a mark.

00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:01.510 align:middle line:90%


00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:04.210 align:middle line:84%
So recall that we have the
positive and negative parts

00:02:04.210 --> 00:02:06.220 align:middle line:90%
of f.

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:12.370 align:middle line:84%
So we can write f equals f plus
minus f minus, where these are

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:14.470 align:middle line:84%
the positive and
negative parts of f,

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:22.560 align:middle line:84%
both non-negative functions so
that the absolute value of f

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:25.605 align:middle line:90%
is equal to f plus plus f minus.

00:02:25.605 --> 00:02:33.270 align:middle line:90%


00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:35.310 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, if
I want to compute

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:38.400 align:middle line:84%
the integral of
the absolute value,

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:45.522 align:middle line:84%
this is equal to the
integral of the positive part

00:02:45.522 --> 00:02:46.480 align:middle line:90%
plus the negative part.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:49.190 align:middle line:84%
Remember, these are both
non-negative functions.

00:02:49.190 --> 00:02:56.765 align:middle line:84%
So let me just recall for you
f plus is equal to max f0.

00:02:56.765 --> 00:02:58.930 align:middle line:90%
f minus is equal to--

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:01.680 align:middle line:90%


00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:08.600 align:middle line:84%
I think d for some reason,
the f plus is always easy.

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:11.670 align:middle line:90%
The f minus is always--

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:13.830 align:middle line:90%
max minus f0.

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:20.980 align:middle line:90%


00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:25.450 align:middle line:84%
So the integral of
the absolute value

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:28.420 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the integral of
f plus plus the integral of f

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:29.080 align:middle line:90%
minus.

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:30.580 align:middle line:84%
These are both
defined because these

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:33.070 align:middle line:84%
are both non-negative
measurable functions.

00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:38.260 align:middle line:90%


00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:40.330 align:middle line:84%
So both non-negative
because this

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:43.390 align:middle line:84%
is a max of two things,
one of them involving 0.

00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:47.455 align:middle line:84%
So that's always bigger
than or equal to 0.

00:03:47.455 --> 00:03:49.580 align:middle line:84%
So these are two non-negative
measurable functions.

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:50.850 align:middle line:90%
These integrals exist.

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:55.340 align:middle line:84%
So this is finite if and only
if both of these two things

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:56.480 align:middle line:90%
are finite.

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:04.380 align:middle line:90%
So thus f is--

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:06.510 align:middle line:84%
instead of saying
Lesbesgue integrable,

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:09.240 align:middle line:84%
I'm just going to
say is integrable.

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:13.680 align:middle line:84%
This is equivalent
to the functions

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:18.887 align:middle line:84%
f plus and f minus, the positive
and negative parts of f,

00:04:18.887 --> 00:04:20.271 align:middle line:90%
are integrable.

00:04:20.271 --> 00:04:26.280 align:middle line:90%


00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:33.570 align:middle line:84%
All right, so with
that remark there,

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:37.220 align:middle line:84%
so if f is a measurable function
from E to a measurable set,

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:41.940 align:middle line:90%
the R is integrable.

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:43.220 align:middle line:90%
So "intbl."

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:47.900 align:middle line:90%
My short form is usually--

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:51.100 align:middle line:90%
you can sound it out.

00:04:51.100 --> 00:05:07.600 align:middle line:84%
If f is Lesbesgue integrable,
then the Lesbesgue integral

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:15.610 align:middle line:84%
of f over E is defined
to be the integral of f

00:05:15.610 --> 00:05:21.460 align:middle line:84%
plus over E minus the
integral of f minus.

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:25.900 align:middle line:84%
Now again, so this is a
bit of new terminology.

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:29.320 align:middle line:84%
This is meaningful because
I'm only defining the integral

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:30.880 align:middle line:84%
for integrable
functions, meaning

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:34.490 align:middle line:84%
this is a finite number,
this is a finite number.

00:05:34.490 --> 00:05:36.460 align:middle line:84%
So I can always subtract
two finite numbers.

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:40.640 align:middle line:90%


00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:45.430 align:middle line:84%
So this is the definition of
being Lesbesgue integrable,

00:05:45.430 --> 00:05:47.040 align:middle line:90%
the Lesbesgue integral.

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:50.780 align:middle line:84%
So what are some
immediate properties

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:54.050 align:middle line:84%
of integrable functions
and the integral?

00:05:54.050 --> 00:06:03.930 align:middle line:84%
Suppose f and g from
E to R are integrable.

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:06.490 align:middle line:90%


00:06:06.490 --> 00:06:14.605 align:middle line:84%
Then for all c in R, the
integral of c times f--

00:06:14.605 --> 00:06:25.050 align:middle line:84%
no, I should say c
times f is integrable.

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:31.360 align:middle line:84%
And the integral of c
times f is equal to c

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:32.740 align:middle line:90%
times the integral of f.

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:36.490 align:middle line:90%


00:06:36.490 --> 00:06:38.110 align:middle line:84%
That's one simple
property that just

00:06:38.110 --> 00:06:42.640 align:middle line:84%
follows from the definition
along with the linearity

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:45.340 align:middle line:84%
that we have for the integral
of non-negative functions

00:06:45.340 --> 00:06:46.615 align:middle line:90%
over non-negative scalars.

00:06:46.615 --> 00:06:49.910 align:middle line:90%


00:06:49.910 --> 00:06:54.770 align:middle line:90%
f plus g is integrable.

00:06:54.770 --> 00:07:00.140 align:middle line:84%
And the integral
of f plus g over E

00:07:00.140 --> 00:07:09.920 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the integral over
E of f plus the integral of g.

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:20.870 align:middle line:84%
And again, just
an analog of what

00:07:20.870 --> 00:07:24.870 align:middle line:84%
we had for the integral
of non-negative functions.

00:07:24.870 --> 00:07:35.910 align:middle line:84%
If A and B are disjoint
measurable sets,

00:07:35.910 --> 00:07:40.290 align:middle line:84%
then integral of
f over A union B

00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:42.990 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the
integral over A of f

00:07:42.990 --> 00:07:48.390 align:middle line:90%
plus the integral over B of f.

00:07:48.390 --> 00:08:01.960 align:middle line:90%


00:08:01.960 --> 00:08:03.460 align:middle line:84%
One I'm just going
to leave to you.

00:08:03.460 --> 00:08:04.520 align:middle line:90%
It's pretty clear.

00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:07.210 align:middle line:90%
I just write c as--

00:08:07.210 --> 00:08:09.610 align:middle line:84%
if it's 0, then this
follows immediately.

00:08:09.610 --> 00:08:11.440 align:middle line:84%
If c is positive,
then it doesn't

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:13.540 align:middle line:84%
change the positive
and negative parts.

00:08:13.540 --> 00:08:16.180 align:middle line:84%
The positive part
of c times f is just

00:08:16.180 --> 00:08:19.258 align:middle line:90%
c times the positive part of f.

00:08:19.258 --> 00:08:21.550 align:middle line:84%
And the negative part is just
c times the negative part

00:08:21.550 --> 00:08:26.480 align:middle line:84%
of f is the negative
part of c times f.

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:28.910 align:middle line:84%
But it flips them
if c is negative.

00:08:28.910 --> 00:08:35.130 align:middle line:84%
So you can just check that
equality for those two cases.

00:08:35.130 --> 00:08:37.380 align:middle line:84%
So let's move on to
something more interesting,

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:39.890 align:middle line:84%
so for example, the
fact that the integral

00:08:39.890 --> 00:08:43.820 align:middle line:90%
is linear in f and g.

00:08:43.820 --> 00:08:48.560 align:middle line:84%
So note that by the
triangle inequality, I have

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:53.960 align:middle line:84%
that f plus g is less
than or equal to f

00:08:53.960 --> 00:08:57.200 align:middle line:84%
plus g, the absolute-- so the
absolute value of f plus g

00:08:57.200 --> 00:08:59.210 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal to
the absolute value of f

00:08:59.210 --> 00:09:00.920 align:middle line:90%
plus the absolute value of g.

00:09:00.920 --> 00:09:04.358 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, by what
we know for the integral

00:09:04.358 --> 00:09:05.900 align:middle line:84%
of non-negative
measurable functions,

00:09:05.900 --> 00:09:10.400 align:middle line:84%
the integral of f
plus g is less than

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:14.960 align:middle line:84%
or equal to the integral
of the absolute value of f

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:17.120 align:middle line:84%
plus the absolute
value of g, which

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:21.830 align:middle line:84%
equals by linearity for
non-negative measurable

00:09:21.830 --> 00:09:25.670 align:middle line:84%
functions is equal to
the sum of the integrals.

00:09:25.670 --> 00:09:27.680 align:middle line:90%
Each of these is finite.

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:29.210 align:middle line:84%
So therefore, their
sum is finite.

00:09:29.210 --> 00:09:32.190 align:middle line:90%


00:09:32.190 --> 00:09:39.380 align:middle line:84%
So if I have two
integrable functions,

00:09:39.380 --> 00:09:40.639 align:middle line:90%
their sum is integrable.

00:09:40.639 --> 00:09:46.330 align:middle line:90%


00:09:46.330 --> 00:09:49.210 align:middle line:84%
Now why is the
integral of the sum

00:09:49.210 --> 00:09:50.770 align:middle line:84%
equal to the sum
of the integrals?

00:09:50.770 --> 00:09:56.320 align:middle line:84%
Well, we have f
plus g, splitting

00:09:56.320 --> 00:10:02.660 align:middle line:84%
f and g into their
positive and negative parts

00:10:02.660 --> 00:10:07.250 align:middle line:84%
and writing f as f
plus minus f minus.

00:10:07.250 --> 00:10:12.170 align:middle line:84%
I can write f plus g as f
plus plus g plus minus f

00:10:12.170 --> 00:10:14.600 align:middle line:90%
minus plus g minus.

00:10:14.600 --> 00:10:18.740 align:middle line:84%
Now this does not say that
the positive part of f plus g

00:10:18.740 --> 00:10:22.130 align:middle line:84%
is equal to this or that the
negative part of f plus g

00:10:22.130 --> 00:10:23.000 align:middle line:90%
is this.

00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:29.450 align:middle line:84%
But it does say that
the positive part

00:10:29.450 --> 00:10:37.620 align:middle line:84%
of f plus g plus the sum of the
negative parts is equal to--

00:10:37.620 --> 00:10:42.150 align:middle line:90%


00:10:42.150 --> 00:10:45.240 align:middle line:84%
just by splitting the left
side into positive and negative

00:10:45.240 --> 00:10:47.430 align:middle line:90%
parts, I get this identity.

00:10:47.430 --> 00:10:51.700 align:middle line:90%


00:10:51.700 --> 00:10:54.640 align:middle line:84%
Now all of these are
non-negative measurable

00:10:54.640 --> 00:10:56.552 align:middle line:90%
functions.

00:10:56.552 --> 00:10:58.760 align:middle line:84%
Everything appearing here
are non-negative measurable

00:10:58.760 --> 00:10:59.690 align:middle line:90%
functions.

00:10:59.690 --> 00:11:01.880 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, the
integral, which

00:11:01.880 --> 00:11:05.030 align:middle line:84%
is linear for non-negative
measurable functions,

00:11:05.030 --> 00:11:12.400 align:middle line:84%
tells me that the integral of
f plus g plus the integral of f

00:11:12.400 --> 00:11:21.870 align:middle line:84%
minus plus g minus equals
the integral of f plus plus g

00:11:21.870 --> 00:11:29.145 align:middle line:84%
plus over E plus the
integral over E plus g minus.

00:11:29.145 --> 00:11:32.130 align:middle line:90%


00:11:32.130 --> 00:11:37.830 align:middle line:90%
Now what do I do is I rearrange.

00:11:37.830 --> 00:11:40.980 align:middle line:84%
I bring this over to this
side, this over to this side.

00:11:40.980 --> 00:11:43.800 align:middle line:84%
And I can still use linearity
because this is an integral

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:45.900 align:middle line:90%
of non-negative functions.

00:11:45.900 --> 00:11:50.430 align:middle line:84%
I get that the
integral of E over f

00:11:50.430 --> 00:11:57.330 align:middle line:84%
plus g plus minus the integral
of E over f plus g minus

00:11:57.330 --> 00:12:00.370 align:middle line:90%
is equal to--

00:12:00.370 --> 00:12:08.200 align:middle line:90%
so let's just do this slowly.

00:12:08.200 --> 00:12:17.660 align:middle line:84%
This is equal to this
integral and this integral.

00:12:17.660 --> 00:12:21.910 align:middle line:84%
So even though we didn't know
that the positive part and--

00:12:21.910 --> 00:12:23.960 align:middle line:84%
equals the sum of
the positive parts,

00:12:23.960 --> 00:12:26.200 align:middle line:84%
so even though we don't
know that the positive part

00:12:26.200 --> 00:12:27.850 align:middle line:84%
of the sum is the sum
of the positive parts

00:12:27.850 --> 00:12:29.558 align:middle line:84%
and the same thing
for the negative part,

00:12:29.558 --> 00:12:31.990 align:middle line:84%
we still get the
integrals equal.

00:12:31.990 --> 00:12:38.630 align:middle line:84%
And this thing right here is
just equal to, by definition,

00:12:38.630 --> 00:12:42.160 align:middle line:90%
the integral of f plus g.

00:12:42.160 --> 00:12:44.020 align:middle line:90%
And this thing over here--

00:12:44.020 --> 00:12:47.950 align:middle line:84%
again, if we use linearity for
the integrals of non-negative

00:12:47.950 --> 00:13:01.040 align:middle line:84%
functions, this is equal to f
plus plus the integral over E

00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:02.500 align:middle line:90%
of g plus, minus--

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:05.170 align:middle line:84%
and again, just
expanding this and then

00:13:05.170 --> 00:13:09.940 align:middle line:84%
carrying through the
minus, minus F minus.

00:13:09.940 --> 00:13:13.660 align:middle line:84%
So you see here, all we're using
is linearity for non-negative

00:13:13.660 --> 00:13:20.330 align:middle line:84%
for a sum of two non-negative
measurable functions.

00:13:20.330 --> 00:13:22.670 align:middle line:84%
And this is just,
again, by the definition

00:13:22.670 --> 00:13:28.780 align:middle line:84%
of the integral the
sum of the integrals.

00:13:28.780 --> 00:13:32.080 align:middle line:90%


00:13:32.080 --> 00:13:36.630 align:middle line:90%
And so that was 2.

00:13:36.630 --> 00:13:37.967 align:middle line:90%
What's the proof of 3?

00:13:37.967 --> 00:13:41.950 align:middle line:90%


00:13:41.950 --> 00:14:04.030 align:middle line:84%
3 from 2 and the fact that if
I take f times the indicator

00:14:04.030 --> 00:14:07.660 align:middle line:84%
function of A union B, this is
equal to f times the indicator

00:14:07.660 --> 00:14:11.710 align:middle line:84%
function of A plus f times
the indicator function of B

00:14:11.710 --> 00:14:13.870 align:middle line:84%
when A and B are
two disjoint sets.

00:14:13.870 --> 00:14:19.860 align:middle line:90%


00:14:19.860 --> 00:14:25.400 align:middle line:84%
So using what we know
about for the integral

00:14:25.400 --> 00:14:27.440 align:middle line:84%
of non-negative
measurable functions,

00:14:27.440 --> 00:14:30.800 align:middle line:84%
it follows that the
integral of f over A union B

00:14:30.800 --> 00:14:33.410 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the integral
of this quantity

00:14:33.410 --> 00:14:38.030 align:middle line:84%
here over E, which is equal
to the sum of the integral

00:14:38.030 --> 00:14:41.280 align:middle line:84%
of this sum here, which is equal
to the sum of the integrals.

00:14:41.280 --> 00:14:43.640 align:middle line:84%
And then going back, that's
equal to the integral

00:14:43.640 --> 00:14:48.650 align:middle line:84%
of f over A plus the
integral of f over B.

00:14:48.650 --> 00:14:55.820 align:middle line:84%
So again, it follows from
linearity, this fact,

00:14:55.820 --> 00:14:57.560 align:middle line:84%
and although I
didn't write it down,

00:14:57.560 --> 00:15:01.160 align:middle line:84%
the fact that even now
for integrable functions,

00:15:01.160 --> 00:15:03.890 align:middle line:84%
but that the
integral of a subset

00:15:03.890 --> 00:15:06.597 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the integral of the
function times the indicator.

00:15:06.597 --> 00:15:08.180 align:middle line:84%
The reason that's
true is because it's

00:15:08.180 --> 00:15:10.970 align:middle line:84%
true for non-negative
measurable functions

00:15:10.970 --> 00:15:17.525 align:middle line:84%
and simply from the definition
of the Lesbesgue integral.

00:15:17.525 --> 00:15:27.240 align:middle line:90%


00:15:27.240 --> 00:15:30.180 align:middle line:84%
All right, so some more
properties of the integral.

00:15:30.180 --> 00:15:34.390 align:middle line:90%


00:15:34.390 --> 00:15:43.570 align:middle line:84%
Suppose f and g from a
measurable subset to R

00:15:43.570 --> 00:15:44.440 align:middle line:90%
are integrable.

00:15:44.440 --> 00:15:49.640 align:middle line:90%


00:15:49.640 --> 00:15:54.140 align:middle line:90%
Then the following holds.

00:15:54.140 --> 00:15:57.260 align:middle line:84%
The integral-- I mean, the
absolute value of the integral

00:15:57.260 --> 00:16:01.370 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal to the
integral of the absolute value.

00:16:01.370 --> 00:16:05.620 align:middle line:90%


00:16:05.620 --> 00:16:18.510 align:middle line:84%
If f equals g almost
everywhere, then--

00:16:18.510 --> 00:16:23.570 align:middle line:90%
so let me-- so let's just--

00:16:23.570 --> 00:16:26.210 align:middle line:84%
I have no idea what
I was going to say.

00:16:26.210 --> 00:16:30.670 align:middle line:84%
I had to cut it down
a little differently.

00:16:30.670 --> 00:16:33.010 align:middle line:90%
So let's restart.

00:16:33.010 --> 00:16:36.640 align:middle line:84%
Suppose f and g are two
functions measurable.

00:16:36.640 --> 00:16:39.301 align:middle line:90%
Need to write measurable.

00:16:39.301 --> 00:16:48.000 align:middle line:84%
OK, back from the top,
if f is integrable,

00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:51.810 align:middle line:84%
then the absolute value
of the integral of f

00:16:51.810 --> 00:16:55.995 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal to the
integral of the absolute value.

00:16:55.995 --> 00:17:00.780 align:middle line:90%


00:17:00.780 --> 00:17:13.310 align:middle line:84%
If g is integrable, and f
equals g almost everywhere,

00:17:13.310 --> 00:17:18.170 align:middle line:90%
then f is integrable.

00:17:18.170 --> 00:17:21.550 align:middle line:90%


00:17:21.550 --> 00:17:25.960 align:middle line:84%
And the integral of f
over E equals the integral

00:17:25.960 --> 00:17:31.540 align:middle line:84%
of g, again, back to that
philosophy from last lecture

00:17:31.540 --> 00:17:37.180 align:middle line:84%
where I said if your conclusions
are in terms of integrals,

00:17:37.180 --> 00:17:42.820 align:middle line:84%
then usually the hypotheses
can be stated only

00:17:42.820 --> 00:17:44.890 align:middle line:84%
in terms of almost
everywhere information.

00:17:44.890 --> 00:17:47.730 align:middle line:90%


00:17:47.730 --> 00:17:59.250 align:middle line:84%
3, if f and g our
integrable, and f

00:17:59.250 --> 00:18:05.640 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal
to g almost everywhere,

00:18:05.640 --> 00:18:08.790 align:middle line:84%
then the integral of
f over E is less than

00:18:08.790 --> 00:18:12.540 align:middle line:90%
or equal to the integral of g.

00:18:12.540 --> 00:18:29.690 align:middle line:90%


00:18:29.690 --> 00:18:34.820 align:middle line:84%
So one follows simply from the
definition and the relationship

00:18:34.820 --> 00:18:36.872 align:middle line:84%
between the absolute
value of f, f,

00:18:36.872 --> 00:18:38.330 align:middle line:84%
and the positive
and negative part.

00:18:38.330 --> 00:18:45.970 align:middle line:84%
So we have the absolute value
of the integral of E of f over E

00:18:45.970 --> 00:18:49.240 align:middle line:84%
of f is equal to, by
definition, the integral

00:18:49.240 --> 00:18:55.660 align:middle line:84%
of the positive part of E
minus the negative part of E.

00:18:55.660 --> 00:18:57.530 align:middle line:84%
Now these are two
non-negative numbers.

00:18:57.530 --> 00:19:00.033 align:middle line:84%
So by the triangle
inequality, the absolute value

00:19:00.033 --> 00:19:02.200 align:middle line:84%
of their difference is less
than or equal to the sum

00:19:02.200 --> 00:19:05.920 align:middle line:84%
of the absolute values,
which are equal to themselves

00:19:05.920 --> 00:19:07.765 align:middle line:84%
because they're
non-negative numbers.

00:19:07.765 --> 00:19:10.390 align:middle line:90%


00:19:10.390 --> 00:19:13.030 align:middle line:90%
And now the integral of--

00:19:13.030 --> 00:19:15.790 align:middle line:84%
or the sum of integrals is equal
to the integral of the sum.

00:19:15.790 --> 00:19:19.620 align:middle line:90%


00:19:19.620 --> 00:19:24.840 align:middle line:84%
And f plus the positive part
plus the negative part of f

00:19:24.840 --> 00:19:29.340 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the
absolute value of f.

00:19:29.340 --> 00:19:31.780 align:middle line:84%
So the triangle
inequality-- or not--

00:19:31.780 --> 00:19:33.280 align:middle line:84%
I shouldn't say
triangle inequality.

00:19:33.280 --> 00:19:38.250 align:middle line:84%
But I mean, if you think
of the integral as the sum,

00:19:38.250 --> 00:19:40.900 align:middle line:84%
then this is a version of
the triangle inequality.

00:19:40.900 --> 00:19:45.780 align:middle line:84%
But the triangle inequality
for Lesbesgue integrals holds.

00:19:45.780 --> 00:19:58.870 align:middle line:84%
So 2, so we have f is
less than or equal to--

00:19:58.870 --> 00:20:01.150 align:middle line:84%
so first off,
absolute value of f

00:20:01.150 --> 00:20:04.090 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the
absolute value of g

00:20:04.090 --> 00:20:12.000 align:middle line:84%
almost everywhere,
which implies what

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:16.062 align:middle line:84%
we know from the integral
of non-negative measurable

00:20:16.062 --> 00:20:18.270 align:middle line:84%
functions, that if I have
two non-negative measurable

00:20:18.270 --> 00:20:21.750 align:middle line:84%
functions who equal each
other almost everywhere,

00:20:21.750 --> 00:20:22.875 align:middle line:90%
then their integrals agree.

00:20:22.875 --> 00:20:28.510 align:middle line:90%


00:20:28.510 --> 00:20:31.460 align:middle line:90%
And that's fine.

00:20:31.460 --> 00:20:32.880 align:middle line:90%
Thus, f is integrable.

00:20:32.880 --> 00:20:41.560 align:middle line:90%


00:20:41.560 --> 00:20:47.320 align:middle line:84%
Now why does the integral of f
over E equal the integral of g

00:20:47.320 --> 00:20:48.100 align:middle line:90%
over E?

00:20:48.100 --> 00:20:52.490 align:middle line:90%


00:20:52.490 --> 00:20:58.360 align:middle line:84%
So since f equals g
almost everywhere,

00:20:58.360 --> 00:21:05.150 align:middle line:84%
f minus g in absolute value is
equal to 0 almost everywhere.

00:21:05.150 --> 00:21:12.630 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, if I look
at the absolute value

00:21:12.630 --> 00:21:15.300 align:middle line:84%
of the difference of
these two integrals,

00:21:15.300 --> 00:21:24.710 align:middle line:84%
this is by linearity equal to
the integral of the difference,

00:21:24.710 --> 00:21:29.410 align:middle line:84%
which by the triangle inequality
for Lesbesgue integrals

00:21:29.410 --> 00:21:33.670 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal to the
integral of the absolute value.

00:21:33.670 --> 00:21:35.620 align:middle line:84%
And by what we know
for the integral

00:21:35.620 --> 00:21:37.360 align:middle line:84%
of non-negative
measurable functions,

00:21:37.360 --> 00:21:40.420 align:middle line:84%
if I have a function which
is 0 almost everywhere, then

00:21:40.420 --> 00:21:44.760 align:middle line:90%
its integral is 0.

00:21:44.760 --> 00:21:49.350 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, the absolute
value of-- or the integral

00:21:49.350 --> 00:21:53.400 align:middle line:84%
of f over E must equal
the integral of g over E.

00:21:53.400 --> 00:21:54.420 align:middle line:90%
So that proves 2.

00:21:54.420 --> 00:21:57.590 align:middle line:90%


00:21:57.590 --> 00:22:04.430 align:middle line:84%
And 3 is-- again, so
we're just using the stuff

00:22:04.430 --> 00:22:07.250 align:middle line:84%
that we know from the integral
of non-negative measurable

00:22:07.250 --> 00:22:08.060 align:middle line:90%
functions.

00:22:08.060 --> 00:22:12.560 align:middle line:90%


00:22:12.560 --> 00:22:23.030 align:middle line:84%
Find a function h of x to be
g of x minus f of x and 0.

00:22:23.030 --> 00:22:24.020 align:middle line:90%
Now when is it this?

00:22:24.020 --> 00:22:36.510 align:middle line:84%
This is when if g of x is bigger
than or equal to f of x, and 0

00:22:36.510 --> 00:22:37.450 align:middle line:90%
otherwise.

00:22:37.450 --> 00:22:41.100 align:middle line:84%
So this is a non-negative
measurable function.

00:22:41.100 --> 00:22:44.640 align:middle line:84%
It's equal to g of x
minus f of x when g of x

00:22:44.640 --> 00:22:47.220 align:middle line:84%
is bigger than or equal to
f of x, and 0 otherwise.

00:22:47.220 --> 00:22:52.010 align:middle line:90%


00:22:52.010 --> 00:22:55.780 align:middle line:84%
And h is a non-negative
measurable function.

00:22:55.780 --> 00:23:00.460 align:middle line:84%
h equals-- since g is bigger
than or equal to f almost

00:23:00.460 --> 00:23:04.810 align:middle line:84%
everywhere, h is
equal to g minus f

00:23:04.810 --> 00:23:06.520 align:middle line:84%
almost everywhere
because this condition

00:23:06.520 --> 00:23:08.230 align:middle line:90%
is satisfied almost everywhere.

00:23:08.230 --> 00:23:28.800 align:middle line:90%


00:23:28.800 --> 00:23:34.370 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, I
get that 0, which

00:23:34.370 --> 00:23:39.020 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal
to the integral over E

00:23:39.020 --> 00:23:41.900 align:middle line:90%
of the positive part of h--

00:23:41.900 --> 00:23:43.970 align:middle line:84%
because this is just a
non-negative function,

00:23:43.970 --> 00:23:46.178 align:middle line:84%
we know what its Lesbesgue
integral is defined to be.

00:23:46.178 --> 00:23:51.060 align:middle line:84%
It's just given by
the previous lecture.

00:23:51.060 --> 00:23:53.480 align:middle line:84%
So it has to be a
non-negative number, which

00:23:53.480 --> 00:23:57.125 align:middle line:84%
because h is non-negative,
this is equal to h.

00:23:57.125 --> 00:23:58.715 align:middle line:90%
h plus is equal to h.

00:23:58.715 --> 00:24:01.370 align:middle line:90%


00:24:01.370 --> 00:24:08.560 align:middle line:84%
But what we proved a minute
ago, since these two functions,

00:24:08.560 --> 00:24:21.700 align:middle line:84%
integrable function,
so I should say why.

00:24:21.700 --> 00:24:24.710 align:middle line:84%
So why am I also asserting that
h is an integrable function?

00:24:24.710 --> 00:24:27.430 align:middle line:84%
Well, the integral of h
is equal to the integral

00:24:27.430 --> 00:24:29.830 align:middle line:84%
of g minus f, which,
if I want to put

00:24:29.830 --> 00:24:32.710 align:middle line:84%
absolute values on things, the
integral of the absolute value

00:24:32.710 --> 00:24:38.980 align:middle line:84%
of h is equal to the difference
of two integral functions,

00:24:38.980 --> 00:24:41.080 align:middle line:84%
which is integrable
almost Everywhere

00:24:41.080 --> 00:24:43.630 align:middle line:84%
and by what we did
for part 2, then

00:24:43.630 --> 00:24:47.720 align:middle line:90%
that tells me h is integrable.

00:24:47.720 --> 00:24:54.030 align:middle line:84%
So the integral of
H, so by 2, must

00:24:54.030 --> 00:25:00.810 align:middle line:84%
be equal to the integral of g
minus f, which by the linearity

00:25:00.810 --> 00:25:03.570 align:middle line:84%
that we proved in
the theorem before is

00:25:03.570 --> 00:25:11.670 align:middle line:84%
equal to the integral of
g minus the integral of f.

00:25:11.670 --> 00:25:14.400 align:middle line:84%
And you remember, we started
all the way at the end of this

00:25:14.400 --> 00:25:17.190 align:middle line:84%
or at the beginning of this
with 0 is less than or equal to.

00:25:17.190 --> 00:25:19.170 align:middle line:84%
So the integral of g
will be bigger than

00:25:19.170 --> 00:25:20.460 align:middle line:90%
or equal to the integral of f.

00:25:20.460 --> 00:25:26.400 align:middle line:90%


00:25:26.400 --> 00:25:35.910 align:middle line:84%
So now the final most useful
convergence theorem one

00:25:35.910 --> 00:25:38.655 align:middle line:84%
encounters in
Lesbesgue integration

00:25:38.655 --> 00:25:41.790 align:middle line:84%
or in integration
theory is Lesbesgue's

00:25:41.790 --> 00:25:43.098 align:middle line:90%
dominated convergence theorem.

00:25:43.098 --> 00:25:45.390 align:middle line:84%
Or I'll just call it the
dominated convergence theorem.

00:25:45.390 --> 00:26:08.248 align:middle line:90%


00:26:08.248 --> 00:26:09.415 align:middle line:90%
Let me make a small comment.

00:26:09.415 --> 00:26:12.560 align:middle line:90%


00:26:12.560 --> 00:26:14.990 align:middle line:84%
Well, let me pause and make
a small comment right here.

00:26:14.990 --> 00:26:21.440 align:middle line:84%
What functions are Lesbesgue
integrable first off?

00:26:21.440 --> 00:26:23.090 align:middle line:84%
What are some
examples of functions

00:26:23.090 --> 00:26:25.190 align:middle line:90%
that are Lesbesgue integrable?

00:26:25.190 --> 00:26:29.251 align:middle line:90%


00:26:29.251 --> 00:26:31.375 align:middle line:90%
Did I-- let's see.

00:26:31.375 --> 00:26:33.127 align:middle line:90%
Did I skip that?

00:26:33.127 --> 00:26:34.210 align:middle line:90%
Oh, I had it at one point.

00:26:34.210 --> 00:26:39.010 align:middle line:90%


00:26:39.010 --> 00:26:42.420 align:middle line:84%
So what sets have
finite measure?

00:26:42.420 --> 00:26:45.110 align:middle line:90%


00:26:45.110 --> 00:26:47.465 align:middle line:90%
We know that the set--

00:26:47.465 --> 00:26:50.270 align:middle line:84%
the measure of intervals is the
length of the intervals, right?

00:26:50.270 --> 00:26:56.690 align:middle line:84%
So any sets that are contained
in a large interval are--

00:26:56.690 --> 00:26:59.960 align:middle line:84%
any measurable subsets that are
contained in a large interval

00:26:59.960 --> 00:27:01.010 align:middle line:90%
have finite measures.

00:27:01.010 --> 00:27:04.705 align:middle line:84%
So compact sets, which we know
are measurable, because they're

00:27:04.705 --> 00:27:08.340 align:middle line:90%
Borel sets, are--

00:27:08.340 --> 00:27:11.474 align:middle line:90%
have finite measure.

00:27:11.474 --> 00:27:15.960 align:middle line:90%
So why am I saying that?

00:27:15.960 --> 00:27:21.750 align:middle line:84%
If I have simple functions which
are nonzero only on sets that

00:27:21.750 --> 00:27:26.970 align:middle line:84%
have finite measure, then that
will be an integrable function,

00:27:26.970 --> 00:27:33.970 align:middle line:84%
because go back to
the definition of how

00:27:33.970 --> 00:27:36.040 align:middle line:84%
one integrates a
simple function, right?

00:27:36.040 --> 00:27:40.320 align:middle line:84%
It's the sum of the
coefficients times the measure

00:27:40.320 --> 00:27:43.300 align:middle line:84%
of the sets, where the--
it takes that coefficient.

00:27:43.300 --> 00:27:47.880 align:middle line:84%
So if I'm only
nonzero on a set of--

00:27:47.880 --> 00:27:51.480 align:middle line:84%
with the convention that
0 times infinity equals 0

00:27:51.480 --> 00:27:57.480 align:middle line:84%
so that it's the measure of
0 times the measure of where

00:27:57.480 --> 00:28:04.060 align:middle line:84%
the function is 0 is equal to
0, then those simple functions

00:28:04.060 --> 00:28:06.970 align:middle line:84%
that are nonzero only on
sets of finite measure

00:28:06.970 --> 00:28:08.560 align:middle line:90%
will be integral functions.

00:28:08.560 --> 00:28:13.880 align:middle line:84%
Now what about
continuous functions?

00:28:13.880 --> 00:28:16.722 align:middle line:90%
So in fact, let me--

00:28:16.722 --> 00:28:18.430 align:middle line:84%
I'm not going to prove
this because we're

00:28:18.430 --> 00:28:21.620 align:middle line:84%
going to prove something
much stronger in a minute.

00:28:21.620 --> 00:28:24.590 align:middle line:84%
So what about
continuous functions

00:28:24.590 --> 00:28:27.320 align:middle line:84%
on a closed and bounded
interval, say a--

00:28:27.320 --> 00:28:28.670 align:middle line:90%
so a continuous function on AB?

00:28:28.670 --> 00:28:31.310 align:middle line:90%


00:28:31.310 --> 00:28:34.070 align:middle line:84%
Let's just talk our way
through why those functions

00:28:34.070 --> 00:28:35.880 align:middle line:90%
are integrable on those sets.

00:28:35.880 --> 00:28:38.160 align:middle line:84%
So a continuous function
on an interval AB--

00:28:38.160 --> 00:28:40.950 align:middle line:90%


00:28:40.950 --> 00:28:43.260 align:middle line:84%
if a function is continuous,
then its absolute value

00:28:43.260 --> 00:28:45.060 align:middle line:90%
is continuous on AB.

00:28:45.060 --> 00:28:46.560 align:middle line:84%
And therefore,
the absolute value

00:28:46.560 --> 00:28:49.680 align:middle line:84%
must be bounded
by some constant.

00:28:49.680 --> 00:28:52.530 align:middle line:84%
A continuous function
attains a minimum and maximum

00:28:52.530 --> 00:28:54.550 align:middle line:84%
on a closed and
bounded interval.

00:28:54.550 --> 00:28:57.270 align:middle line:84%
So the absolute value
of a continuous function

00:28:57.270 --> 00:29:00.225 align:middle line:84%
on a closed and bounded interval
is bounded by some constant.

00:29:00.225 --> 00:29:03.020 align:middle line:90%


00:29:03.020 --> 00:29:06.650 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, by monotonicity
of Lesbesgue integrals,

00:29:06.650 --> 00:29:10.640 align:middle line:84%
the integral of the absolute
value of f over the interval AB

00:29:10.640 --> 00:29:13.010 align:middle line:84%
will be less than or equal
to some constant times

00:29:13.010 --> 00:29:15.930 align:middle line:90%
the interval over the--

00:29:15.930 --> 00:29:20.610 align:middle line:84%
or times-- will be less than
or equal to the integral

00:29:20.610 --> 00:29:22.680 align:middle line:90%
of the constant over AB.

00:29:22.680 --> 00:29:24.570 align:middle line:84%
And the integral
of a constant is

00:29:24.570 --> 00:29:28.050 align:middle line:84%
just equal to the constant
times a measure of the set.

00:29:28.050 --> 00:29:31.830 align:middle line:84%
Just by how we define the
integral of simple functions,

00:29:31.830 --> 00:29:36.960 align:middle line:84%
a constant is just the simplest
of simple functions, which

00:29:36.960 --> 00:29:41.130 align:middle line:84%
would be the constant bounding
above the absolute value

00:29:41.130 --> 00:29:45.180 align:middle line:84%
times the measure of the closed
and bounded interval AB, which

00:29:45.180 --> 00:29:46.500 align:middle line:90%
is B minus a

00:29:46.500 --> 00:29:49.990 align:middle line:84%
So the Lesbesgue integral
of a continuous function--

00:29:49.990 --> 00:29:52.290 align:middle line:90%
so what was all that for?

00:29:52.290 --> 00:29:54.600 align:middle line:84%
To say that a
continuous function

00:29:54.600 --> 00:29:58.465 align:middle line:84%
on a closed and bounded interval
is a Lesbesgue integrable.

00:29:58.465 --> 00:30:00.090 align:middle line:84%
Now we're about to
show something right

00:30:00.090 --> 00:30:02.850 align:middle line:84%
after I prove the state and
prove the dominated convergence

00:30:02.850 --> 00:30:06.240 align:middle line:84%
theorem, which is much
stronger than that,

00:30:06.240 --> 00:30:07.982 align:middle line:84%
is that in fact, the
Lesbesgue integral

00:30:07.982 --> 00:30:10.440 align:middle line:84%
of a continuous function on
the closed and bounded interval

00:30:10.440 --> 00:30:12.730 align:middle line:90%
equals its Riemann integral.

00:30:12.730 --> 00:30:16.050 align:middle line:84%
So in fact, for
continuous functions,

00:30:16.050 --> 00:30:18.330 align:middle line:84%
you already know how to
compute Lesbesgue integrals.

00:30:18.330 --> 00:30:21.310 align:middle line:84%
They're equal to the
Riemann integrals.

00:30:21.310 --> 00:30:24.285 align:middle line:84%
So back to the dominated
convergence theorem.

00:30:24.285 --> 00:30:27.030 align:middle line:90%


00:30:27.030 --> 00:30:44.870 align:middle line:84%
Let g be an integrable
function over E. f

00:30:44.870 --> 00:30:56.860 align:middle line:84%
in a sequence of measurable
functions such that--

00:30:56.860 --> 00:31:02.360 align:middle line:90%


00:31:02.360 --> 00:31:05.030 align:middle line:90%
two things.

00:31:05.030 --> 00:31:10.045 align:middle line:84%
One, for all n, the
absolute value of fn

00:31:10.045 --> 00:31:11.170 align:middle line:90%
is less than or equal to g.

00:31:11.170 --> 00:31:12.190 align:middle line:90%
So g is non-negative.

00:31:12.190 --> 00:31:14.216 align:middle line:84%
I guess I should have
put here non-negative.

00:31:14.216 --> 00:31:20.080 align:middle line:90%


00:31:20.080 --> 00:31:37.170 align:middle line:84%
And almost everywhere,
there exists an f from E

00:31:37.170 --> 00:31:46.130 align:middle line:84%
to R such that fn
converges to f pointwise

00:31:46.130 --> 00:31:54.520 align:middle line:84%
is almost everywhere, so
meaning fn of x converges to f

00:31:54.520 --> 00:32:05.330 align:middle line:90%
of x for almost every x in E.

00:32:05.330 --> 00:32:10.520 align:middle line:84%
So these, the sequence
of measurable functions,

00:32:10.520 --> 00:32:11.880 align:middle line:90%
converge to--

00:32:11.880 --> 00:32:14.000 align:middle line:84%
pointwise to this function
almost everywhere.

00:32:14.000 --> 00:32:17.970 align:middle line:84%
And they're all dominated
by an integrable function.

00:32:17.970 --> 00:32:29.660 align:middle line:84%
Then the conclusion is that
the limit of the integrals

00:32:29.660 --> 00:32:33.892 align:middle line:84%
equals the integral
of the limits

00:32:33.892 --> 00:32:35.100 align:middle line:90%
or the integral of the limit.

00:32:35.100 --> 00:32:45.210 align:middle line:90%


00:32:45.210 --> 00:32:54.470 align:middle line:84%
So this is a very useful
and powerful theorem

00:32:54.470 --> 00:32:55.550 align:middle line:90%
in integration.

00:32:55.550 --> 00:33:04.050 align:middle line:84%
It's way stronger than you can--
than anything one can really

00:33:04.050 --> 00:33:09.780 align:middle line:84%
say in Riemann integration, as
far as convergence theorems go.

00:33:09.780 --> 00:33:12.510 align:middle line:84%
Riemann integration
requires always

00:33:12.510 --> 00:33:15.480 align:middle line:84%
in some form, some form of
uniform convergence here.

00:33:15.480 --> 00:33:18.030 align:middle line:84%
All we have is pointwise
convergence almost everywhere,

00:33:18.030 --> 00:33:18.810 align:middle line:90%
right?

00:33:18.810 --> 00:33:21.890 align:middle line:84%
And the second
requirement-- so remember,

00:33:21.890 --> 00:33:24.390 align:middle line:84%
the monotone convergence theorem
also required the functions

00:33:24.390 --> 00:33:27.860 align:middle line:90%
to be increasing.

00:33:27.860 --> 00:33:31.640 align:middle line:84%
So here, now we're
dealing not just

00:33:31.640 --> 00:33:34.310 align:middle line:84%
with non-negative functions,
just arbitrary measurable

00:33:34.310 --> 00:33:36.860 align:middle line:90%
functions.

00:33:36.860 --> 00:33:53.010 align:middle line:84%
Then I should say real
valued measurable functions

00:33:53.010 --> 00:33:54.870 align:middle line:84%
because our measurable
functions could also

00:33:54.870 --> 00:33:55.890 align:middle line:90%
be extended real value.

00:33:55.890 --> 00:33:57.520 align:middle line:84%
But I wanted this
to be real value.

00:33:57.520 --> 00:34:00.360 align:middle line:84%
So all we need on top
of pointwise convergence

00:34:00.360 --> 00:34:03.000 align:middle line:84%
almost everywhere
is just for these

00:34:03.000 --> 00:34:08.010 align:middle line:84%
to be bounded above by some
integrable function, some fixed

00:34:08.010 --> 00:34:09.190 align:middle line:90%
integrable function.

00:34:09.190 --> 00:34:12.030 align:middle line:84%
Then we conclude that the
limit of the integrals

00:34:12.030 --> 00:34:15.750 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the integral
of the limit, which

00:34:15.750 --> 00:34:21.120 align:middle line:84%
is an extremely powerful and
useful theorem in analysis.

00:34:21.120 --> 00:34:25.820 align:middle line:90%


00:34:25.820 --> 00:34:28.070 align:middle line:84%
So we'll prove it
using Fatou's lemma.

00:34:28.070 --> 00:34:34.900 align:middle line:84%
But first, again,
the conclusions

00:34:34.900 --> 00:34:36.370 align:middle line:90%
are about integrals.

00:34:36.370 --> 00:34:38.679 align:middle line:84%
And I'm making almost
everywhere statements

00:34:38.679 --> 00:34:42.190 align:middle line:84%
in the assumptions, which I said
you could always basically do.

00:34:42.190 --> 00:34:44.560 align:middle line:84%
Let me briefly reduce
ourselves to the case

00:34:44.560 --> 00:34:47.920 align:middle line:84%
that these two things
hold almost everywhere.

00:34:47.920 --> 00:34:49.300 align:middle line:90%
I mean everywhere.

00:34:49.300 --> 00:34:54.429 align:middle line:84%
Since for all n, fn
is less than or equal

00:34:54.429 --> 00:35:02.260 align:middle line:84%
to g almost everywhere,
this implies that for all n,

00:35:02.260 --> 00:35:08.190 align:middle line:90%
fn is integrable.

00:35:08.190 --> 00:35:14.460 align:middle line:90%


00:35:14.460 --> 00:35:23.310 align:middle line:84%
So moreover, fn converges
to f almost everywhere

00:35:23.310 --> 00:35:26.490 align:middle line:84%
implies a couple
of things, that--

00:35:26.490 --> 00:35:29.520 align:middle line:84%
remember, the pointwise even
almost everywhere convergence

00:35:29.520 --> 00:35:32.620 align:middle line:84%
of measurable functions
is a measurable function.

00:35:32.620 --> 00:35:37.380 align:middle line:90%
So f is measurable.

00:35:37.380 --> 00:35:51.000 align:middle line:84%
And so f is less than or equal
to g almost everywhere, which

00:35:51.000 --> 00:35:59.059 align:middle line:90%
implies that f is integrable.

00:35:59.059 --> 00:36:04.110 align:middle line:90%


00:36:04.110 --> 00:36:20.060 align:middle line:84%
So since changing
fn for each n--

00:36:20.060 --> 00:36:22.250 align:middle line:84%
or I should say--
let me say this.

00:36:22.250 --> 00:36:29.910 align:middle line:90%
Since changing f on a--

00:36:29.910 --> 00:36:34.966 align:middle line:90%


00:36:34.966 --> 00:36:35.674 align:middle line:90%
what am I saying?

00:36:35.674 --> 00:36:50.180 align:middle line:84%
Since changing f and fn for
all n on a set of measure

00:36:50.180 --> 00:37:05.015 align:middle line:90%
0 does not affect the integrals.

00:37:05.015 --> 00:37:06.390 align:middle line:84%
In the end, our
conclusion should

00:37:06.390 --> 00:37:10.500 align:middle line:84%
be about the limit of the
integral of the sequence equals

00:37:10.500 --> 00:37:14.250 align:middle line:90%
the integral of f.

00:37:14.250 --> 00:37:18.660 align:middle line:84%
And since if I change
fn and f for each n

00:37:18.660 --> 00:37:23.940 align:middle line:84%
on a set of measure 0, doesn't
change the integrals, right?

00:37:23.940 --> 00:37:27.040 align:middle line:84%
We can assume that
those two assumptions--

00:37:27.040 --> 00:37:28.650 align:middle line:90%
we can assume that these two--

00:37:28.650 --> 00:37:37.880 align:middle line:90%


00:37:37.880 --> 00:37:47.980 align:middle line:84%
we can assume that these two
assumptions hold everywhere.

00:37:47.980 --> 00:37:59.810 align:middle line:90%


00:37:59.810 --> 00:38:20.290 align:middle line:90%
And these hold everywhere on E.

00:38:20.290 --> 00:38:22.210 align:middle line:84%
So first off, I want
to note-- so now let's

00:38:22.210 --> 00:38:23.560 align:middle line:90%
actually get to the proof.

00:38:23.560 --> 00:38:25.310 align:middle line:84%
All right, maybe you
need to take a second

00:38:25.310 --> 00:38:29.300 align:middle line:90%
to think about what I said here.

00:38:29.300 --> 00:38:33.010 align:middle line:84%
But the point is that
because the conclusions are

00:38:33.010 --> 00:38:35.770 align:middle line:84%
in terms of integrals,
and although I stated it

00:38:35.770 --> 00:38:41.110 align:middle line:84%
for almost everywhere,
I can fiddle

00:38:41.110 --> 00:38:46.240 align:middle line:84%
with fn and f for each n
on a fixed set of measure 0

00:38:46.240 --> 00:38:49.310 align:middle line:90%
without affecting the integrals.

00:38:49.310 --> 00:38:52.870 align:middle line:84%
So if I want to prove
this, I can change them

00:38:52.870 --> 00:38:56.320 align:middle line:84%
on a set of measure 0 without
affecting the integrals.

00:38:56.320 --> 00:39:00.040 align:middle line:84%
And on the set of measures 0, I
make it so that all of the fns

00:39:00.040 --> 00:39:00.670 align:middle line:90%
equal f.

00:39:00.670 --> 00:39:04.330 align:middle line:84%
And so then I have this
convergence everywhere.

00:39:04.330 --> 00:39:07.540 align:middle line:84%
And I change f to be 0 on that
set as well so that I will

00:39:07.540 --> 00:39:09.490 align:middle line:90%
have this everywhere as well.

00:39:09.490 --> 00:39:12.780 align:middle line:90%


00:39:12.780 --> 00:39:16.680 align:middle line:84%
But so if you need a
second, if you'd like,

00:39:16.680 --> 00:39:19.380 align:middle line:84%
just imagine that I erased
that "almost everywhere"

00:39:19.380 --> 00:39:20.880 align:middle line:84%
so that the statement
of the theorem

00:39:20.880 --> 00:39:22.650 align:middle line:84%
has these things
holding everywhere.

00:39:22.650 --> 00:39:26.802 align:middle line:84%
And then think a
little bit about why

00:39:26.802 --> 00:39:28.260 align:middle line:84%
I don't need it to
hold everywhere,

00:39:28.260 --> 00:39:29.930 align:middle line:90%
just almost everywhere.

00:39:29.930 --> 00:39:33.010 align:middle line:90%


00:39:33.010 --> 00:39:36.270 align:middle line:84%
So we assume for all n, fn
is less than or equal to g.

00:39:36.270 --> 00:39:40.160 align:middle line:84%
There exists an f
so that I have that.

00:39:40.160 --> 00:39:53.030 align:middle line:84%
So note, for all n, the
integral of f sub n is less than

00:39:53.030 --> 00:39:58.380 align:middle line:84%
or equal to the integral
of the absolute value,

00:39:58.380 --> 00:40:06.290 align:middle line:84%
which is less than or
equal to the integral of g,

00:40:06.290 --> 00:40:13.100 align:middle line:84%
which implies that the sequence
formed by the integrals

00:40:13.100 --> 00:40:16.970 align:middle line:84%
of these guys-- so this is
a sequence of real numbers--

00:40:16.970 --> 00:40:25.760 align:middle line:84%
is a bounded sequence
of real numbers.

00:40:25.760 --> 00:40:28.470 align:middle line:90%


00:40:28.470 --> 00:40:31.560 align:middle line:84%
So it has a limsup
and a liminf, right?

00:40:31.560 --> 00:40:34.740 align:middle line:84%
Now remember from
your first analysis

00:40:34.740 --> 00:40:42.990 align:middle line:84%
course, whatever it was, that
the limit of a bounded sequence

00:40:42.990 --> 00:40:46.320 align:middle line:84%
is equal to L if and only if
the liminf and limsup equal

00:40:46.320 --> 00:40:48.900 align:middle line:84%
each other, and they equal
L. So what we're going to do

00:40:48.900 --> 00:40:51.870 align:middle line:84%
is we're going to show that
the liminf and the limsup

00:40:51.870 --> 00:40:54.510 align:middle line:84%
of this sequence of
numbers are equal.

00:40:54.510 --> 00:40:56.200 align:middle line:84%
And they equal
the integral of f.

00:40:56.200 --> 00:40:59.155 align:middle line:90%


00:40:59.155 --> 00:41:01.490 align:middle line:84%
And to do that, we're
going to use Fatou's lemma.

00:41:01.490 --> 00:41:11.105 align:middle line:84%
So since g plus or
minus f sub n is less--

00:41:11.105 --> 00:41:15.085 align:middle line:84%
or is bigger than or equal to
0 because the absolute value

00:41:15.085 --> 00:41:16.960 align:middle line:84%
of f sub n is always
less than or equal to g.

00:41:16.960 --> 00:41:19.120 align:middle line:84%
So g plus or minus
f sub n is always

00:41:19.120 --> 00:41:20.590 align:middle line:90%
bigger than or equal to 0.

00:41:20.590 --> 00:41:29.040 align:middle line:84%
I can now apply Fatou's
lemma, which tells me

00:41:29.040 --> 00:41:32.310 align:middle line:84%
that for a sequence of
non-negative measurable

00:41:32.310 --> 00:41:35.340 align:middle line:84%
functions, the integral
of the liminf is less than

00:41:35.340 --> 00:41:37.200 align:middle line:84%
or equal to the liminf
of the integrals.

00:41:37.200 --> 00:41:41.780 align:middle line:90%


00:41:41.780 --> 00:41:53.510 align:middle line:84%
So again, so the fns
are converging to f.

00:41:53.510 --> 00:41:56.090 align:middle line:90%
So f of x--

00:41:56.090 --> 00:42:02.270 align:middle line:84%
fn of x for all-- so fix x in
E, fn of x converges to f of x.

00:42:02.270 --> 00:42:06.650 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, liminf of
fn of x equals f of x.

00:42:06.650 --> 00:42:13.710 align:middle line:84%
So the liminf of g
minus f equals fn.

00:42:13.710 --> 00:42:17.820 align:middle line:84%
Sorry, equals g
minus f because fns

00:42:17.820 --> 00:42:21.180 align:middle line:84%
converge to f, again,
for each pointwise.

00:42:21.180 --> 00:42:28.580 align:middle line:90%


00:42:28.580 --> 00:42:29.840 align:middle line:90%
Let's do a minus here.

00:42:29.840 --> 00:42:32.590 align:middle line:90%


00:42:32.590 --> 00:42:37.570 align:middle line:84%
And by Fatou's lemma, this is
less than or equal to liminf

00:42:37.570 --> 00:42:40.720 align:middle line:84%
as n goes to infinity
of the integral of E--

00:42:40.720 --> 00:42:43.930 align:middle line:90%
over E of g minus f sub n.

00:42:43.930 --> 00:42:48.330 align:middle line:84%
Now this is equal
to the integral.

00:42:48.330 --> 00:42:51.810 align:middle line:84%
Using linearity, this is
equal to the integral of g

00:42:51.810 --> 00:42:55.590 align:middle line:90%
minus the integral of f sub n.

00:42:55.590 --> 00:42:57.900 align:middle line:84%
When I take the liminf of
that and carry it through,

00:42:57.900 --> 00:43:03.080 align:middle line:84%
I get the liminf when it hits
a minus turns into a limsup.

00:43:03.080 --> 00:43:11.910 align:middle line:90%


00:43:11.910 --> 00:43:14.340 align:middle line:84%
So whenever I have a
bounded sequence of numbers,

00:43:14.340 --> 00:43:18.000 align:middle line:84%
the liminf minus that
sequence of numbers

00:43:18.000 --> 00:43:22.300 align:middle line:84%
equals minus the limsup of
that sequence of numbers.

00:43:22.300 --> 00:43:31.040 align:middle line:84%
So here I'm using liminf
of minus An, n being--

00:43:31.040 --> 00:43:36.170 align:middle line:90%
equals minus limsup of An.

00:43:36.170 --> 00:43:44.080 align:middle line:90%


00:43:44.080 --> 00:43:54.280 align:middle line:84%
And similarly, I get that
the integral of g plus f

00:43:54.280 --> 00:43:58.540 align:middle line:84%
is less than or
equal to just by--

00:43:58.540 --> 00:44:00.490 align:middle line:84%
now if I choose lemma
again, but now I

00:44:00.490 --> 00:44:01.960 align:middle line:84%
don't have to
switch minuses, this

00:44:01.960 --> 00:44:08.260 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal to the
integral of g plus liminf

00:44:08.260 --> 00:44:13.300 align:middle line:84%
as n goes to infinity of
the integral of fn of E.

00:44:13.300 --> 00:44:16.840 align:middle line:90%
So I have two inequalities.

00:44:16.840 --> 00:44:25.400 align:middle line:90%
I have that.

00:44:25.400 --> 00:44:28.370 align:middle line:84%
And I should say it's less
than or equal to, so maybe

00:44:28.370 --> 00:44:31.220 align:middle line:90%
include a little bit of that.

00:44:31.220 --> 00:44:34.640 align:middle line:84%
And then I also have
this inequality here.

00:44:34.640 --> 00:44:45.390 align:middle line:90%


00:44:45.390 --> 00:44:49.110 align:middle line:84%
Now all of these quantities
that I've written down,

00:44:49.110 --> 00:44:50.520 align:middle line:90%
these are all finite numbers.

00:44:50.520 --> 00:44:51.670 align:middle line:90%
This is one reason why.

00:44:51.670 --> 00:44:54.540 align:middle line:84%
So this is the limsup of
this bounded sequence.

00:44:54.540 --> 00:44:55.360 align:middle line:90%
That's a number.

00:44:55.360 --> 00:44:56.805 align:middle line:90%
This is a number.

00:44:56.805 --> 00:44:57.700 align:middle line:90%
That's a number.

00:44:57.700 --> 00:45:00.630 align:middle line:84%
So I can subtract and
move them to either side

00:45:00.630 --> 00:45:02.550 align:middle line:90%
of this inequality.

00:45:02.550 --> 00:45:04.050 align:middle line:84%
So there's no funny
business going

00:45:04.050 --> 00:45:06.300 align:middle line:90%
on with subtracting infinities.

00:45:06.300 --> 00:45:08.530 align:middle line:90%
This is all on the level.

00:45:08.530 --> 00:45:12.240 align:middle line:84%
So moving the limsup
over and subtracting over

00:45:12.240 --> 00:45:18.930 align:middle line:84%
the integral of g minus f,
I get that the limsup as n

00:45:18.930 --> 00:45:24.420 align:middle line:84%
goes to infinity of
f sub n is less than

00:45:24.420 --> 00:45:33.440 align:middle line:84%
or equal to the
integral of g minus f.

00:45:33.440 --> 00:45:37.940 align:middle line:84%
And now by linearity, that's
equal to the integral of f.

00:45:37.940 --> 00:45:42.230 align:middle line:84%
And this is also equal
to, by linearity--

00:45:42.230 --> 00:45:51.260 align:middle line:84%
let's see-- minus
the integral of g.

00:45:51.260 --> 00:45:57.950 align:middle line:84%
And by the second
yellow box here, this

00:45:57.950 --> 00:46:00.170 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal
to the integral of g

00:46:00.170 --> 00:46:06.320 align:middle line:84%
plus the liminf of
the fn, so liminf

00:46:06.320 --> 00:46:07.655 align:middle line:90%
of the integrals of the fns.

00:46:07.655 --> 00:46:10.890 align:middle line:90%


00:46:10.890 --> 00:46:13.680 align:middle line:90%
So what do I have?

00:46:13.680 --> 00:46:20.220 align:middle line:84%
I have the limsup is less than
or equal to the integral of f,

00:46:20.220 --> 00:46:23.310 align:middle line:84%
is less than or
equal to the liminf.

00:46:23.310 --> 00:46:27.210 align:middle line:84%
The liminf always
sits below the limsup.

00:46:27.210 --> 00:46:28.620 align:middle line:84%
So therefore,
those three numbers

00:46:28.620 --> 00:46:31.930 align:middle line:90%
have to equal each other.

00:46:31.930 --> 00:46:36.210 align:middle line:84%
So this box always
sits below this box.

00:46:36.210 --> 00:46:38.940 align:middle line:84%
So all three numbers must
equal each other then--

00:46:38.940 --> 00:46:47.180 align:middle line:90%


00:46:47.180 --> 00:46:56.660 align:middle line:84%
equals the integral of
f equals liminf as n

00:46:56.660 --> 00:47:00.480 align:middle line:90%
goes to infinity of fn.

00:47:00.480 --> 00:47:02.940 align:middle line:84%
And I bet in
analysis, you thought

00:47:02.940 --> 00:47:07.230 align:middle line:84%
limsups and liminfs would
never be useful, but they are.

00:47:07.230 --> 00:47:12.730 align:middle line:90%


00:47:12.730 --> 00:47:17.740 align:middle line:84%
So that is the proof of the
dominated convergence theorem.

00:47:17.740 --> 00:47:23.635 align:middle line:84%
Now let's use some of this
muscle we've been building up.

00:47:23.635 --> 00:47:27.150 align:middle line:90%


00:47:27.150 --> 00:47:33.740 align:middle line:84%
So suppose a is less than b
and f is a continuous function

00:47:33.740 --> 00:47:34.445 align:middle line:90%
on a, b.

00:47:34.445 --> 00:47:40.850 align:middle line:90%


00:47:40.850 --> 00:47:45.675 align:middle line:84%
Then the Lesbesgue
integral over a,

00:47:45.675 --> 00:47:54.095 align:middle line:84%
b of f is equal to the
Riemann integral of f.

00:47:54.095 --> 00:47:56.670 align:middle line:90%


00:47:56.670 --> 00:48:01.420 align:middle line:90%
This is the Riemann integral.

00:48:01.420 --> 00:48:04.470 align:middle line:90%


00:48:04.470 --> 00:48:06.470 align:middle line:84%
So in the course of
this proof, we'll

00:48:06.470 --> 00:48:12.335 align:middle line:84%
also see why f is,
in fact, integrable.

00:48:12.335 --> 00:48:20.100 align:middle line:90%


00:48:20.100 --> 00:48:20.615 align:middle line:90%
So, proof.

00:48:20.615 --> 00:48:28.170 align:middle line:90%


00:48:28.170 --> 00:48:32.240 align:middle line:84%
So we first show f is
Lesbesgue integrable.

00:48:32.240 --> 00:48:42.650 align:middle line:84%
So this implies that
the absolute value of f

00:48:42.650 --> 00:48:45.620 align:middle line:90%
is also a continuous function.

00:48:45.620 --> 00:48:49.370 align:middle line:84%
And every continuous function
on a closed and bounded interval

00:48:49.370 --> 00:48:50.400 align:middle line:90%
is bounded.

00:48:50.400 --> 00:49:00.590 align:middle line:84%
So there exists a constant
b such that f is--

00:49:00.590 --> 00:49:02.210 align:middle line:84%
the absolute value
of f is less than

00:49:02.210 --> 00:49:06.385 align:middle line:84%
or equal to b on this
closed and bounded interval.

00:49:06.385 --> 00:49:11.420 align:middle line:90%


00:49:11.420 --> 00:49:16.610 align:middle line:84%
Then the integral of
the absolute value

00:49:16.610 --> 00:49:19.910 align:middle line:84%
of f, the Lesbesgue integral
of f of the absolute value of f

00:49:19.910 --> 00:49:26.540 align:middle line:84%
over a, b, this is less
than or equal to a times b,

00:49:26.540 --> 00:49:29.750 align:middle line:84%
the integral over
a, b of capital P.

00:49:29.750 --> 00:49:32.360 align:middle line:84%
And this is the simplest
of simple functions.

00:49:32.360 --> 00:49:34.370 align:middle line:84%
The Lesbesgue integral
of this is just

00:49:34.370 --> 00:49:45.370 align:middle line:84%
b times the measure of b of a,
b, equals b times b minus a,

00:49:45.370 --> 00:49:47.310 align:middle line:90%
which is finite.

00:49:47.310 --> 00:49:50.730 align:middle line:84%
So continuous functions
are Lesbesgue integrable

00:49:50.730 --> 00:49:52.560 align:middle line:84%
on a closed and
bounded interval.

00:49:52.560 --> 00:50:01.340 align:middle line:90%


00:50:01.340 --> 00:50:06.170 align:middle line:90%
Thus, f is Lesbesgue integrable.

00:50:06.170 --> 00:50:18.240 align:middle line:90%


00:50:18.240 --> 00:50:27.450 align:middle line:84%
Now the positive part of f
is a continuous function.

00:50:27.450 --> 00:50:31.400 align:middle line:84%
And the negative part of f
is a continuous function.

00:50:31.400 --> 00:50:33.460 align:middle line:84%
So in fact, you can
write these down

00:50:33.460 --> 00:50:35.890 align:middle line:84%
a little bit differently than
I wrote them down before.

00:50:35.890 --> 00:50:37.240 align:middle line:90%
f plus is equal to--

00:50:37.240 --> 00:50:46.030 align:middle line:90%


00:50:46.030 --> 00:50:48.957 align:middle line:90%
let's see.

00:50:48.957 --> 00:50:50.790 align:middle line:84%
So these are the positive
and negative parts

00:50:50.790 --> 00:50:52.860 align:middle line:90%
written slightly differently.

00:50:52.860 --> 00:50:55.350 align:middle line:84%
If f is continuous,
both of these functions

00:50:55.350 --> 00:50:59.460 align:middle line:84%
are continuous
non-negative functions.

00:50:59.460 --> 00:51:04.170 align:middle line:84%
And the Riemann integral of
f plus minus f minus, which

00:51:04.170 --> 00:51:07.200 align:middle line:84%
is f, the integral of f, is
equal to the integral of f

00:51:07.200 --> 00:51:09.600 align:middle line:84%
plus minus the
integral of f minus,

00:51:09.600 --> 00:51:14.160 align:middle line:84%
which is exactly how also
Lesbesgue integrals are defined

00:51:14.160 --> 00:51:16.710 align:middle line:84%
in terms of the integral,
Lesbesgue integral of f

00:51:16.710 --> 00:51:18.420 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the
Lesbesgue integral

00:51:18.420 --> 00:51:23.000 align:middle line:84%
of f plus minus Lesbesgue
integral of f minus.

00:51:23.000 --> 00:51:34.699 align:middle line:84%
If I consider simply these
two cases separately,

00:51:34.699 --> 00:51:41.360 align:middle line:84%
be considering these
separately and showing

00:51:41.360 --> 00:51:47.060 align:middle line:84%
the integral over a,
b, f plus or minus

00:51:47.060 --> 00:51:53.130 align:middle line:84%
equals the corresponding
Riemann integral.

00:51:53.130 --> 00:51:58.770 align:middle line:90%


00:51:58.770 --> 00:52:16.500 align:middle line:84%
And using linearity, I may
assume that f is non-negative.

00:52:16.500 --> 00:52:18.050 align:middle line:90%
So what's the point here?

00:52:18.050 --> 00:52:22.520 align:middle line:84%
I'm trying to show this for
general continuous functions.

00:52:22.520 --> 00:52:25.880 align:middle line:84%
But by splitting it into its
positive and negative parts,

00:52:25.880 --> 00:52:29.690 align:middle line:84%
it suffices to prove
this equals that

00:52:29.690 --> 00:52:31.910 align:middle line:84%
for the positive and
negative parts, both of which

00:52:31.910 --> 00:52:34.350 align:middle line:84%
are continuous functions
and non-negative.

00:52:34.350 --> 00:52:38.300 align:middle line:84%
So I only need to prove
what I want for the case

00:52:38.300 --> 00:52:40.130 align:middle line:90%
that f is non-negative.

00:52:40.130 --> 00:52:42.610 align:middle line:90%
That's the point.

00:52:42.610 --> 00:52:48.240 align:middle line:84%
All right, so we now have
a non-negative continuous

00:52:48.240 --> 00:52:49.230 align:middle line:90%
function on a, b.

00:52:49.230 --> 00:52:51.540 align:middle line:84%
And we want to show the
Lesbesgue integral is

00:52:51.540 --> 00:52:55.550 align:middle line:84%
equal to the Riemann integral
of that continuous function.

00:52:55.550 --> 00:53:08.840 align:middle line:90%
So let xn be--

00:53:08.840 --> 00:53:09.455 align:middle line:90%
let's see.

00:53:09.455 --> 00:53:12.170 align:middle line:90%


00:53:12.170 --> 00:53:14.770 align:middle line:90%
So this should equal a.

00:53:14.770 --> 00:53:16.840 align:middle line:90%
This should equal b--

00:53:16.840 --> 00:53:30.510 align:middle line:84%
be a sequence of
partitions of a,

00:53:30.510 --> 00:53:40.970 align:middle line:84%
b such that the norm of--
so this is just notation

00:53:40.970 --> 00:53:43.850 align:middle line:90%
from back in real analysis.

00:53:43.850 --> 00:53:48.020 align:middle line:84%
You shouldn't take
this as an actual norm.

00:53:48.020 --> 00:53:50.630 align:middle line:84%
Well, I guess it is a
norm in a certain sense.

00:53:50.630 --> 00:53:56.600 align:middle line:84%
But this is just a subset of
a, b that partitions a, b such

00:53:56.600 --> 00:54:00.920 align:middle line:84%
that this quantity here, which
I denote using the norm--

00:54:00.920 --> 00:54:05.010 align:middle line:84%
but don't confuse us with
norms that we discussed before,

00:54:05.010 --> 00:54:07.325 align:middle line:84%
which is defined
to be the max of--

00:54:07.325 --> 00:54:11.360 align:middle line:90%


00:54:11.360 --> 00:54:13.580 align:middle line:90%
and m could change with n.

00:54:13.580 --> 00:54:20.010 align:middle line:90%


00:54:20.010 --> 00:54:21.930 align:middle line:90%
Goes to 0.

00:54:21.930 --> 00:54:29.160 align:middle line:84%
So why am I taking a sequence
of partitions of a, b?

00:54:29.160 --> 00:54:31.830 align:middle line:84%
Because this is how you
compute the Riemann integral

00:54:31.830 --> 00:54:33.840 align:middle line:90%
in terms of Riemann sums.

00:54:33.840 --> 00:54:36.758 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to show that
the sequence of Riemann sums

00:54:36.758 --> 00:54:38.550 align:middle line:84%
converging to the
Riemann integral actually

00:54:38.550 --> 00:54:40.133 align:middle line:84%
converges to the
Lesbesgue integral as

00:54:40.133 --> 00:54:45.540 align:middle line:84%
well, but along a certain
sequence of Riemann sums.

00:54:45.540 --> 00:54:48.760 align:middle line:90%


00:54:48.760 --> 00:54:58.850 align:middle line:84%
So let xi j, n in one
of the subpartitions.

00:54:58.850 --> 00:55:02.020 align:middle line:90%


00:55:02.020 --> 00:55:05.320 align:middle line:84%
So f is a non-negative
continuous function

00:55:05.320 --> 00:55:08.870 align:middle line:90%
on this interval.

00:55:08.870 --> 00:55:11.710 align:middle line:84%
So it has a minimum that
it achieves at some point.

00:55:11.710 --> 00:55:23.920 align:middle line:90%


00:55:23.920 --> 00:55:28.360 align:middle line:90%
Equals f of xi j, n.

00:55:28.360 --> 00:55:33.340 align:middle line:84%
So on the subinterval,
f of x is always bigger

00:55:33.340 --> 00:55:35.560 align:middle line:90%
than or equal to f of xi n, j.

00:55:35.560 --> 00:55:38.900 align:middle line:90%


00:55:38.900 --> 00:55:43.210 align:middle line:84%
So this is how I'm
defining the xi n.

00:55:43.210 --> 00:55:47.920 align:middle line:90%


00:55:47.920 --> 00:56:03.020 align:middle line:84%
Then by the theory of
Riemann integration,

00:56:03.020 --> 00:56:08.390 align:middle line:84%
if I look at limit
as n goes to infinity

00:56:08.390 --> 00:56:12.215 align:middle line:84%
of the associated Riemann
sums, this limit exists.

00:56:12.215 --> 00:56:21.200 align:middle line:90%


00:56:21.200 --> 00:56:30.520 align:middle line:84%
And you get the Riemann integral
of f for a continuous function.

00:56:30.520 --> 00:56:35.050 align:middle line:84%
This should have been covered
in your introductory analysis

00:56:35.050 --> 00:56:36.145 align:middle line:90%
class that--

00:56:36.145 --> 00:56:38.860 align:middle line:90%


00:56:38.860 --> 00:56:41.950 align:middle line:84%
if you like, this is the
lower Riemann integral

00:56:41.950 --> 00:56:43.540 align:middle line:90%
or lower Riemann sum.

00:56:43.540 --> 00:56:47.470 align:middle line:84%
And that as long as you're
going along a partition

00:56:47.470 --> 00:56:50.650 align:middle line:84%
so that this quantity
here is going to 0,

00:56:50.650 --> 00:56:53.595 align:middle line:84%
then the associated
Riemann sums converge

00:56:53.595 --> 00:56:54.595 align:middle line:90%
to the Riemann integral.

00:56:54.595 --> 00:56:59.330 align:middle line:90%


00:56:59.330 --> 00:57:04.795 align:middle line:84%
All right, now each of
these is a finite set.

00:57:04.795 --> 00:57:10.130 align:middle line:90%


00:57:10.130 --> 00:57:14.800 align:middle line:84%
Let n be the union
of these sets.

00:57:14.800 --> 00:57:21.570 align:middle line:90%


00:57:21.570 --> 00:57:24.570 align:middle line:84%
Then this is a countable
union of finite sets.

00:57:24.570 --> 00:57:26.745 align:middle line:90%
So it's countable.

00:57:26.745 --> 00:57:31.340 align:middle line:84%
A countable union of
countable sets is countable.

00:57:31.340 --> 00:57:36.860 align:middle line:84%
In particular, this means
the measure of this set is 0.

00:57:36.860 --> 00:57:40.910 align:middle line:84%
So if I-- the set of
all partition points

00:57:40.910 --> 00:57:47.270 align:middle line:84%
as I range over all of the
partitions converging to 0-- so

00:57:47.270 --> 00:57:49.070 align:middle line:84%
I just took any
sequence of partitions

00:57:49.070 --> 00:57:51.497 align:middle line:84%
with this quantity
here going to 0.

00:57:51.497 --> 00:57:53.330 align:middle line:84%
If I take the union of
all these partitions,

00:57:53.330 --> 00:57:54.440 align:middle line:90%
I get a countable set.

00:57:54.440 --> 00:57:56.780 align:middle line:84%
That set has measure 0
because it's countable.

00:57:56.780 --> 00:58:06.120 align:middle line:90%


00:58:06.120 --> 00:58:08.820 align:middle line:84%
Why am I making this point
that it has measure 0?

00:58:08.820 --> 00:58:12.795 align:middle line:84%
Well, because off of
this set, magic happens.

00:58:12.795 --> 00:58:21.210 align:middle line:90%


00:58:21.210 --> 00:58:26.490 align:middle line:84%
And what we've learned is
that magic happening off

00:58:26.490 --> 00:58:32.190 align:middle line:84%
of a set of measure 0 means
magic happens for integrals.

00:58:32.190 --> 00:58:45.110 align:middle line:90%


00:58:45.110 --> 00:58:47.360 align:middle line:90%
So let me--

00:58:47.360 --> 00:58:49.400 align:middle line:84%
I'd like one more important
piece of information

00:58:49.400 --> 00:58:52.640 align:middle line:84%
that we have from
the theory of Riemann

00:58:52.640 --> 00:58:57.155 align:middle line:84%
integration that the Riemann
sums converge to a Riemann

00:58:57.155 --> 00:58:57.655 align:middle line:90%
integral.

00:58:57.655 --> 00:59:05.470 align:middle line:90%


00:59:05.470 --> 00:59:08.530 align:middle line:84%
Let fn be the following
simple function.

00:59:08.530 --> 00:59:13.630 align:middle line:84%
This is sum from
j equals 1 to mn

00:59:13.630 --> 00:59:21.200 align:middle line:84%
of f of c in j of
the indicator-- times

00:59:21.200 --> 00:59:35.220 align:middle line:84%
the indicator function
of xjn j minus 1 xjn.

00:59:35.220 --> 00:59:43.825 align:middle line:84%
And then could put plus 0 times
the indicator function of xjn.

00:59:43.825 --> 00:59:47.100 align:middle line:90%


00:59:47.100 --> 00:59:48.833 align:middle line:84%
I mean, this part
doesn't really matter.

00:59:48.833 --> 00:59:49.500 align:middle line:90%
I'm just saying.

00:59:49.500 --> 00:59:53.490 align:middle line:84%
So this is a simple
function for each n.

00:59:53.490 --> 01:00:02.455 align:middle line:90%


01:00:02.455 --> 01:00:04.205 align:middle line:84%
Should say a non-negative
simple function.

01:00:04.205 --> 01:00:13.650 align:middle line:90%


01:00:13.650 --> 01:00:18.480 align:middle line:90%
So what's happening now?

01:00:18.480 --> 01:00:20.400 align:middle line:90%
And why did I choose the xi n?

01:00:20.400 --> 01:00:22.515 align:middle line:84%
So let me draw the picture
that goes with this.

01:00:22.515 --> 01:00:26.450 align:middle line:90%


01:00:26.450 --> 01:00:31.310 align:middle line:90%
I have my function f on a, b.

01:00:31.310 --> 01:00:35.510 align:middle line:84%
And what I'm doing
is I'm cutting up

01:00:35.510 --> 01:00:43.525 align:middle line:84%
the domain to get the
approximate Riemann integral.

01:00:43.525 --> 01:01:00.157 align:middle line:90%


01:01:00.157 --> 01:01:01.115 align:middle line:90%
So that should connect.

01:01:01.115 --> 01:01:04.900 align:middle line:90%


01:01:04.900 --> 01:01:07.990 align:middle line:84%
And I'm choosing the heights
to be the minimum of f

01:01:07.990 --> 01:01:10.480 align:middle line:90%
on each of these intervals.

01:01:10.480 --> 01:01:19.060 align:middle line:84%
So this is a f of xi 1,
if you like, f of xi 2.

01:01:19.060 --> 01:01:21.850 align:middle line:84%
And at least for this
picture, this is xi 1.

01:01:21.850 --> 01:01:26.460 align:middle line:90%


01:01:26.460 --> 01:01:30.950 align:middle line:84%
And what I know is as I'm making
this partition finer and finer,

01:01:30.950 --> 01:01:38.020 align:middle line:84%
these approximate areas here are
converging to the full Riemann

01:01:38.020 --> 01:01:40.730 align:middle line:90%
integral of f.

01:01:40.730 --> 01:01:43.020 align:middle line:84%
Another way to
think about that is

01:01:43.020 --> 01:01:48.880 align:middle line:84%
that what these are
just the Lesbesgue

01:01:48.880 --> 01:01:51.250 align:middle line:84%
integral of certain
simple functions, where

01:01:51.250 --> 01:01:55.060 align:middle line:84%
the simple function is 1 on
this and has height f of xi

01:01:55.060 --> 01:01:57.650 align:middle line:90%
in or xi 1.

01:01:57.650 --> 01:02:00.250 align:middle line:84%
This quantity here,
the integral-- the area

01:02:00.250 --> 01:02:03.280 align:middle line:84%
underneath this-- is equal
to the Lesbesgue integral

01:02:03.280 --> 01:02:09.110 align:middle line:84%
of f of xi 2 times the indicator
function of this interval,

01:02:09.110 --> 01:02:10.160 align:middle line:90%
and so on.

01:02:10.160 --> 01:02:16.670 align:middle line:84%
So I can view these pieces
here that are entering

01:02:16.670 --> 01:02:20.030 align:middle line:84%
in the Riemann sum as
Lesbesgue integrals

01:02:20.030 --> 01:02:21.350 align:middle line:90%
of certain simple functions.

01:02:21.350 --> 01:02:23.930 align:middle line:84%
Or I can view this
entire quantity

01:02:23.930 --> 01:02:29.330 align:middle line:84%
as the Lesbesgue integral of
a simple function for reach n.

01:02:29.330 --> 01:02:31.880 align:middle line:84%
Now the goal here
is what we'll do

01:02:31.880 --> 01:02:35.810 align:middle line:84%
is we'll show that the
simple functions whose--

01:02:35.810 --> 01:02:39.080 align:middle line:84%
I mean, in fact, we can
do just do this now.

01:02:39.080 --> 01:02:48.850 align:middle line:84%
Note, for all n, if I look at
the Lesbesgue integral of fn

01:02:48.850 --> 01:02:54.940 align:middle line:84%
over a, b, this is equal to
sum from j equals 1 to m sub n.

01:02:54.940 --> 01:02:57.940 align:middle line:84%
This is a set of measure 0,
so it doesn't contribute.

01:02:57.940 --> 01:02:59.703 align:middle line:90%
f of xi jn--

01:02:59.703 --> 01:03:01.120 align:middle line:84%
these are all
non-negative numbers

01:03:01.120 --> 01:03:06.650 align:middle line:84%
because f is non-negative--
times the measure of xj

01:03:06.650 --> 01:03:19.920 align:middle line:90%
minus 1n, xjn times xjn.

01:03:19.920 --> 01:03:23.800 align:middle line:84%
Now remember, we built
up Lesbesgue integration

01:03:23.800 --> 01:03:25.400 align:middle line:84%
so that the measure
of an interval

01:03:25.400 --> 01:03:26.650 align:middle line:90%
is the length of the interval.

01:03:26.650 --> 01:03:31.320 align:middle line:84%
So this is equal to
xjn minus xj minus 1n.

01:03:31.320 --> 01:03:36.900 align:middle line:84%
So the integral, the
Lesbesgue integral,

01:03:36.900 --> 01:03:44.810 align:middle line:84%
of each of these functions,
each of these simple functions,

01:03:44.810 --> 01:03:51.920 align:middle line:84%
equals that Riemann sum
appearing in this limit, right?

01:03:51.920 --> 01:03:57.020 align:middle line:84%
And now the goal is to
show that the fns converge

01:03:57.020 --> 01:03:59.090 align:middle line:84%
to f almost everywhere
and are bounded

01:03:59.090 --> 01:04:02.070 align:middle line:84%
above by an integral function
at least almost everywhere.

01:04:02.070 --> 01:04:04.670 align:middle line:84%
Then I can apply the
dominated convergence theorem

01:04:04.670 --> 01:04:07.010 align:middle line:84%
to conclude that
the limit n m goes

01:04:07.010 --> 01:04:11.840 align:middle line:84%
to infinity of this thing equals
the Lesbesgue integral of f.

01:04:11.840 --> 01:04:14.780 align:middle line:84%
But this, the limit as n goes
to infinity of this thing,

01:04:14.780 --> 01:04:16.850 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the limit
as n goes to infinity

01:04:16.850 --> 01:04:20.840 align:middle line:84%
of this quantity, which is
equal to the Riemann integral.

01:04:20.840 --> 01:04:22.385 align:middle line:90%
And that's the game plan.

01:04:22.385 --> 01:04:27.880 align:middle line:90%


01:04:27.880 --> 01:04:29.750 align:middle line:84%
And I mean, you can
already guess from here.

01:04:29.750 --> 01:04:32.050 align:middle line:84%
What's the function that
sits above all of the fns?

01:04:32.050 --> 01:04:35.950 align:middle line:90%


01:04:35.950 --> 01:04:40.426 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be at
least away from these--

01:04:40.426 --> 01:04:44.160 align:middle line:84%
at least away from
possibly the endpoints.

01:04:44.160 --> 01:04:44.955 align:middle line:90%
It's going to be f.

01:04:44.955 --> 01:04:47.910 align:middle line:90%


01:04:47.910 --> 01:04:54.870 align:middle line:84%
Then for all xn
a, b, take away n.

01:04:54.870 --> 01:04:58.350 align:middle line:90%
A couple of things I have.

01:04:58.350 --> 01:05:06.785 align:middle line:84%
Well, first thing is less
than or equal to f of x.

01:05:06.785 --> 01:05:10.980 align:middle line:90%


01:05:10.980 --> 01:05:15.120 align:middle line:84%
Now I'm going to show
that on a, b, take away

01:05:15.120 --> 01:05:18.600 align:middle line:84%
n, which is a set of
measure 0, that fn of x

01:05:18.600 --> 01:05:19.800 align:middle line:90%
converges to f of x.

01:05:19.800 --> 01:05:22.550 align:middle line:90%


01:05:22.550 --> 01:05:23.240 align:middle line:90%
So now I claim--

01:05:23.240 --> 01:05:32.740 align:middle line:90%


01:05:32.740 --> 01:05:43.220 align:middle line:84%
then-- i.e., fn converges
to f almost everywhere.

01:05:43.220 --> 01:05:46.420 align:middle line:84%
And it's bounded above by a
Lesbesgue integral function

01:05:46.420 --> 01:05:47.330 align:middle line:90%
almost everywhere.

01:05:47.330 --> 01:05:49.750 align:middle line:84%
So then we can apply the
dominated convergence theorem

01:05:49.750 --> 01:05:50.980 align:middle line:90%
to get what we want.

01:05:50.980 --> 01:05:52.330 align:middle line:90%
So let's prove this.

01:05:52.330 --> 01:05:56.890 align:middle line:90%


01:05:56.890 --> 01:06:01.455 align:middle line:84%
So let xb and ab take away the
set of all partition points.

01:06:01.455 --> 01:06:04.770 align:middle line:90%


01:06:04.770 --> 01:06:06.110 align:middle line:90%
So we want to show this.

01:06:06.110 --> 01:06:08.420 align:middle line:84%
Let's just go back
to a basic epsilon.

01:06:08.420 --> 01:06:10.760 align:middle line:84%
In argument, let
epsilon be positive.

01:06:10.760 --> 01:06:14.970 align:middle line:90%


01:06:14.970 --> 01:06:24.530 align:middle line:84%
Since f is continuous
at x, there

01:06:24.530 --> 01:06:33.230 align:middle line:84%
exists a delta positive
so that if x minus y

01:06:33.230 --> 01:06:42.000 align:middle line:84%
is less than delta,
then f of x minus f of y

01:06:42.000 --> 01:06:42.875 align:middle line:90%
is less than epsilon.

01:06:42.875 --> 01:06:46.240 align:middle line:90%


01:06:46.240 --> 01:06:48.880 align:middle line:84%
Now we know that the partitions
are getting finer and finer,

01:06:48.880 --> 01:06:49.380 align:middle line:90%
right?

01:06:49.380 --> 01:06:54.330 align:middle line:84%
So x is in a, b
take away n, right?

01:06:54.330 --> 01:07:02.040 align:middle line:84%
So since the norms of these
partitions, which remember,

01:07:02.040 --> 01:07:02.775 align:middle line:90%
is the max--

01:07:02.775 --> 01:07:14.750 align:middle line:90%


01:07:14.750 --> 01:07:16.930 align:middle line:84%
I forgot that n when
I was writing it here.

01:07:16.930 --> 01:07:18.520 align:middle line:90%
And this-- should be n.

01:07:18.520 --> 01:07:22.740 align:middle line:90%


01:07:22.740 --> 01:07:30.720 align:middle line:84%
Since this goes to 0,
there exist capital M

01:07:30.720 --> 01:07:35.670 align:middle line:84%
so that all n bigger than
or equal to capital M,

01:07:35.670 --> 01:07:42.560 align:middle line:84%
this quantity here,
the length of--

01:07:42.560 --> 01:07:44.570 align:middle line:84%
the longest length
of the subintervals--

01:07:44.570 --> 01:07:48.760 align:middle line:90%


01:07:48.760 --> 01:07:50.660 align:middle line:90%
is less than delta.

01:07:50.660 --> 01:07:52.130 align:middle line:84%
So now let me draw
you a picture.

01:07:52.130 --> 01:07:55.660 align:middle line:84%
So x is in a, b, take
away the partitions,

01:07:55.660 --> 01:07:59.720 align:middle line:84%
all the possible partition
points, all the xj's, n's,

01:07:59.720 --> 01:08:00.220 align:middle line:90%
right?

01:08:00.220 --> 01:08:03.050 align:middle line:90%


01:08:03.050 --> 01:08:11.680 align:middle line:84%
So let-- I claim now
that fn of x minus f of x

01:08:11.680 --> 01:08:12.760 align:middle line:90%
is less than epsilon.

01:08:12.760 --> 01:08:15.880 align:middle line:90%


01:08:15.880 --> 01:08:26.309 align:middle line:84%
Now how do I evaluate for x
in a, b take away n fn of x?

01:08:26.309 --> 01:08:29.580 align:middle line:90%


01:08:29.580 --> 01:08:34.475 align:middle line:90%
And fn of x is equal to--

01:08:34.475 --> 01:08:43.220 align:middle line:90%


01:08:43.220 --> 01:08:51.245 align:middle line:84%
times the indicator function
of xj minus 1n, xjn of x.

01:08:51.245 --> 01:08:53.870 align:middle line:84%
And I don't have to worry about
the endpoint, because remember,

01:08:53.870 --> 01:08:58.940 align:middle line:84%
x is in a, b, take away all
of the partition points.

01:08:58.940 --> 01:09:03.819 align:middle line:90%


01:09:03.819 --> 01:09:07.590 align:middle line:84%
xjn is always a partition
point. xjn is always

01:09:07.590 --> 01:09:11.370 align:middle line:84%
b, so I'm always
taking away b and a.

01:09:11.370 --> 01:09:12.930 align:middle line:90%
So this must equal--

01:09:12.930 --> 01:09:15.279 align:middle line:84%
this x must lie in one
of these intervals,

01:09:15.279 --> 01:09:19.050 align:middle line:84%
but not be one of
the partition points.

01:09:19.050 --> 01:09:21.569 align:middle line:90%
So this must equal--

01:09:21.569 --> 01:09:23.160 align:middle line:90%
this is bad notation.

01:09:23.160 --> 01:09:25.709 align:middle line:90%
But let's say fxe, OK, k--

01:09:25.709 --> 01:09:28.560 align:middle line:90%


01:09:28.560 --> 01:09:52.450 align:middle line:84%
for the unique k, such that
x is in xk minus 1n xk.

01:09:52.450 --> 01:10:01.090 align:middle line:90%


01:10:01.090 --> 01:10:14.630 align:middle line:84%
Then since xi n, k
is in this interval,

01:10:14.630 --> 01:10:19.430 align:middle line:84%
and the max over the length
of the small intervals,

01:10:19.430 --> 01:10:23.300 align:middle line:84%
which is the differences
here, is less than delta--

01:10:23.300 --> 01:10:24.940 align:middle line:90%
so in particular for this one.

01:10:24.940 --> 01:10:27.990 align:middle line:90%


01:10:27.990 --> 01:10:30.750 align:middle line:90%
So I have-- here's a picture.

01:10:30.750 --> 01:10:35.610 align:middle line:90%
Here's xk minus 1n, xnk.

01:10:35.610 --> 01:10:38.370 align:middle line:90%


01:10:38.370 --> 01:10:41.670 align:middle line:90%
Xi nk is somewhere in there.

01:10:41.670 --> 01:10:43.050 align:middle line:90%
x is somewhere in there.

01:10:43.050 --> 01:10:46.350 align:middle line:90%


01:10:46.350 --> 01:10:49.440 align:middle line:84%
And since this quantity
here is less than delta,

01:10:49.440 --> 01:11:01.060 align:middle line:84%
this implies that x minus xi
in k must be less than delta.

01:11:01.060 --> 01:11:07.840 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, f
of x minus f xi f

01:11:07.840 --> 01:11:17.350 align:middle line:84%
sub n of x, which is equal
to f of x minus f of xi kn.

01:11:17.350 --> 01:11:21.790 align:middle line:84%
Now these are within delta
distance to each other.

01:11:21.790 --> 01:11:25.060 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, f of x
and f of that number

01:11:25.060 --> 01:11:27.430 align:middle line:84%
must be within
epsilon of each other

01:11:27.430 --> 01:11:29.305 align:middle line:90%
simply by how delta was chosen.

01:11:29.305 --> 01:11:33.540 align:middle line:90%


01:11:33.540 --> 01:11:36.600 align:middle line:84%
So we've shown that for all n
bigger than or equal to capital

01:11:36.600 --> 01:11:41.190 align:middle line:84%
M, f of x minus fn of
x is less than epsilon.

01:11:41.190 --> 01:11:43.800 align:middle line:90%


01:11:43.800 --> 01:11:48.330 align:middle line:84%
Thus, limit as n
goes to infinity

01:11:48.330 --> 01:11:56.850 align:middle line:84%
of fn of x equals f
of x, all x in a, b,

01:11:56.850 --> 01:11:59.130 align:middle line:90%
take away the partition points.

01:11:59.130 --> 01:12:14.250 align:middle line:90%


01:12:14.250 --> 01:12:24.730 align:middle line:90%
So we have a couple of things.

01:12:24.730 --> 01:12:27.540 align:middle line:84%
So I said this, but now I'm
just going to write down what.

01:12:27.540 --> 01:12:30.900 align:middle line:90%


01:12:30.900 --> 01:12:32.130 align:middle line:90%
We have these two things.

01:12:32.130 --> 01:12:39.850 align:middle line:84%
We have that almost everywhere,
fn's are converging to f.

01:12:39.850 --> 01:12:42.460 align:middle line:84%
Almost everywhere, fn is
less than or equal to f.

01:12:42.460 --> 01:12:45.490 align:middle line:84%
f is a fixed continuous
function that's integrable.

01:12:45.490 --> 01:12:47.630 align:middle line:90%
It's non-negative.

01:12:47.630 --> 01:12:54.750 align:middle line:84%
So by the dominated
convergence theorem,

01:12:54.750 --> 01:12:59.370 align:middle line:84%
the integral,
Lesbesgue integral,

01:12:59.370 --> 01:13:03.210 align:middle line:84%
of this function,
continuous function f of x,

01:13:03.210 --> 01:13:06.450 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the limit
as n goes to infinity

01:13:06.450 --> 01:13:13.070 align:middle line:84%
of the Lesbesgue integrals of
these special simple functions,

01:13:13.070 --> 01:13:25.930 align:middle line:84%
which, as we computed right
here, is equal to the limit

01:13:25.930 --> 01:13:29.680 align:middle line:84%
as n goes to infinity
of k equals 1 to m

01:13:29.680 --> 01:13:38.500 align:middle line:84%
sub n, f of xi in j minus
xj and minus xj minus 1n.

01:13:38.500 --> 01:13:47.560 align:middle line:90%
And by how this--

01:13:47.560 --> 01:13:49.410 align:middle line:84%
what we know about
Riemann integrals,

01:13:49.410 --> 01:13:51.060 align:middle line:84%
this converges to
the Riemann integral.

01:13:51.060 --> 01:13:58.170 align:middle line:90%


01:13:58.170 --> 01:14:03.372 align:middle line:84%
And thus the Lesbesgue integral
equals the Riemann integral

01:14:03.372 --> 01:14:04.455 align:middle line:90%
for a continuous function.

01:14:04.455 --> 01:14:14.670 align:middle line:90%


01:14:14.670 --> 01:14:19.050 align:middle line:84%
So we have discussed
the Riemann integral

01:14:19.050 --> 01:14:23.460 align:middle line:84%
of real-valued
measurable functions

01:14:23.460 --> 01:14:28.570 align:middle line:84%
or real-valued
integrable functions.

01:14:28.570 --> 01:14:33.220 align:middle line:84%
Now quite often, we want to
have complex-valued functions

01:14:33.220 --> 01:14:37.150 align:middle line:84%
defined on measurable
subsets of real numbers.

01:14:37.150 --> 01:14:39.920 align:middle line:90%
What do we do then?

01:14:39.920 --> 01:14:41.860 align:middle line:84%
Well, everything that
we've done basically

01:14:41.860 --> 01:14:44.980 align:middle line:84%
applies as long
as it makes sense.

01:14:44.980 --> 01:14:47.920 align:middle line:90%
So let me state that here.

01:14:47.920 --> 01:14:56.230 align:middle line:90%


01:14:56.230 --> 01:14:58.320 align:middle line:84%
So all of the previous
theorems that we've

01:14:58.320 --> 01:15:14.900 align:middle line:84%
proven, as long as
they make sense,

01:15:14.900 --> 01:15:18.380 align:middle line:84%
you can use what we've done
for real-valued integrable

01:15:18.380 --> 01:15:21.530 align:middle line:84%
functions to what we call
complex-valued integrable

01:15:21.530 --> 01:15:37.430 align:middle line:84%
function imply the
corresponding statements

01:15:37.430 --> 01:15:51.740 align:middle line:84%
for complex-valued
integrable functions.

01:15:51.740 --> 01:15:52.835 align:middle line:90%
And what are these?

01:15:52.835 --> 01:15:56.830 align:middle line:90%


01:15:56.830 --> 01:16:02.800 align:middle line:84%
So f from a measurable subset
of E to now the complex numbers.

01:16:02.800 --> 01:16:06.625 align:middle line:90%


01:16:06.625 --> 01:16:13.360 align:middle line:90%
So let me say E to--

01:16:13.360 --> 01:16:17.230 align:middle line:84%
and now let me just define what
I mean by complex integrable

01:16:17.230 --> 01:16:20.115 align:middle line:90%
functions, meaning--

01:16:20.115 --> 01:16:24.300 align:middle line:90%


01:16:24.300 --> 01:16:35.840 align:middle line:84%
so f from a measurable subset of
E to C is Lesbesgue integrable

01:16:35.840 --> 01:16:44.670 align:middle line:84%
if the same condition
holds, that the integral

01:16:44.670 --> 01:16:48.270 align:middle line:90%
of the absolute value of f is--

01:16:48.270 --> 01:16:50.730 align:middle line:84%
or now this is a modulus
of a complex number

01:16:50.730 --> 01:16:52.930 align:middle line:90%
for each x-- is integrable.

01:16:52.930 --> 01:16:57.760 align:middle line:84%
So this is a non-negative
measurable function now

01:16:57.760 --> 01:17:01.210 align:middle line:84%
which is defined on E. So this
quantity here makes sense.

01:17:01.210 --> 01:17:03.690 align:middle line:84%
So we say a
complex-valued function

01:17:03.690 --> 01:17:05.985 align:middle line:84%
is Lesbesgue integrable if
this quantity is finite.

01:17:05.985 --> 01:17:08.570 align:middle line:90%


01:17:08.570 --> 01:17:20.140 align:middle line:84%
And now the definition
of the Lesbesgue integral

01:17:20.140 --> 01:17:23.590 align:middle line:84%
of a complex-valued
integrable function

01:17:23.590 --> 01:17:31.340 align:middle line:84%
is defined to be simply the
integral of the real part of f,

01:17:31.340 --> 01:17:37.370 align:middle line:84%
which is, again, a real-valued
integrable function,

01:17:37.370 --> 01:17:42.920 align:middle line:84%
plus i times the integral
of the imaginary part of f.

01:17:42.920 --> 01:17:49.460 align:middle line:84%
So again, we just-- to define
complex integrable functions

01:17:49.460 --> 01:17:51.890 align:middle line:84%
or complex-valued
integrable functions,

01:17:51.890 --> 01:17:53.810 align:middle line:84%
we just take the
same definition.

01:17:53.810 --> 01:17:56.150 align:middle line:84%
And then we define
the integral to be

01:17:56.150 --> 01:18:00.440 align:middle line:84%
the integral of the real part
plus i times the integral

01:18:00.440 --> 01:18:01.560 align:middle line:90%
of the imaginary part.

01:18:01.560 --> 01:18:03.740 align:middle line:84%
And the fact that
this is finite implies

01:18:03.740 --> 01:18:06.890 align:middle line:84%
that these two real-valued
measurable functions

01:18:06.890 --> 01:18:08.644 align:middle line:90%
are, in fact, integrable.

01:18:08.644 --> 01:18:13.290 align:middle line:90%


01:18:13.290 --> 01:18:17.790 align:middle line:84%
And all of the theorems that we
stated before, as long as they

01:18:17.790 --> 01:18:20.445 align:middle line:90%
make sense, carry over.

01:18:20.445 --> 01:18:25.020 align:middle line:84%
For example, linearity of
the integral with respect

01:18:25.020 --> 01:18:28.110 align:middle line:84%
to now multiplication by
complex numbers carries over.

01:18:28.110 --> 01:18:30.420 align:middle line:84%
The integral of the sum is
the sum of the integrals.

01:18:30.420 --> 01:18:37.600 align:middle line:84%
That still carries over just
by using this definition,

01:18:37.600 --> 01:18:41.680 align:middle line:84%
along with the fact that we
know that the integral of two--

01:18:41.680 --> 01:18:44.470 align:middle line:84%
the sum of two real-valued
functions is integrable.

01:18:44.470 --> 01:18:48.730 align:middle line:84%
And the Lesbesgue dominated
convergence theorem then

01:18:48.730 --> 01:18:49.480 align:middle line:90%
carries--

01:18:49.480 --> 01:18:53.620 align:middle line:84%
can be generalized to
complex-valued integrable

01:18:53.620 --> 01:18:55.210 align:middle line:90%
functions.

01:18:55.210 --> 01:18:59.380 align:middle line:84%
Maybe what's not
so clear is the--

01:18:59.380 --> 01:19:01.420 align:middle line:84%
or let me just at least
give you the flavor

01:19:01.420 --> 01:19:04.360 align:middle line:84%
of how you can use what you know
about real-valued integrable

01:19:04.360 --> 01:19:07.897 align:middle line:84%
functions to get
corresponding statements

01:19:07.897 --> 01:19:09.480 align:middle line:84%
for complex-valued
integral functions.

01:19:09.480 --> 01:19:14.330 align:middle line:84%
So let's do the triangle
inequality for integrals,

01:19:14.330 --> 01:19:14.830 align:middle line:90%
right?

01:19:14.830 --> 01:19:22.550 align:middle line:84%
So if I have a complex
integrable or complex-valued

01:19:22.550 --> 01:19:24.160 align:middle line:90%
integral--

01:19:24.160 --> 01:19:32.820 align:middle line:84%
complex-valued integrable
function-- then the integral

01:19:32.820 --> 01:19:35.880 align:middle line:84%
of E of f-- so this is
now a complex number.

01:19:35.880 --> 01:19:41.890 align:middle line:84%
Taking the modulus of that is
less than or equal to that.

01:19:41.890 --> 01:19:47.940 align:middle line:90%
So what's the proof?

01:19:47.940 --> 01:19:49.660 align:middle line:90%
So this is clear.

01:19:49.660 --> 01:19:58.430 align:middle line:84%
So if the integral
of E equals 0.

01:19:58.430 --> 01:20:00.120 align:middle line:84%
So that's just
clear automatically.

01:20:00.120 --> 01:20:01.625 align:middle line:84%
So let's assume
it's not equal to 0.

01:20:01.625 --> 01:20:09.960 align:middle line:90%


01:20:09.960 --> 01:20:15.720 align:middle line:84%
And let alpha be the following
complex number, integral

01:20:15.720 --> 01:20:18.860 align:middle line:90%
of E over--

01:20:18.860 --> 01:20:22.390 align:middle line:90%


01:20:22.390 --> 01:20:26.590 align:middle line:84%
so the integral of f over
E is a complex number.

01:20:26.590 --> 01:20:27.970 align:middle line:90%
I take its complex conjugate.

01:20:27.970 --> 01:20:29.770 align:middle line:90%
I divide by the modulus of that.

01:20:29.770 --> 01:20:35.890 align:middle line:90%


01:20:35.890 --> 01:20:41.020 align:middle line:90%
Then alpha and modulus equals 1.

01:20:41.020 --> 01:20:43.650 align:middle line:90%


01:20:43.650 --> 01:20:51.080 align:middle line:84%
And the absolute value of
the integral of f-- this

01:20:51.080 --> 01:20:59.570 align:middle line:84%
is equal to alpha times
the integral of f over E

01:20:59.570 --> 01:21:02.210 align:middle line:84%
because the complex
conjugate times this

01:21:02.210 --> 01:21:04.820 align:middle line:84%
gives me the modulus squared
divided by the modulus.

01:21:04.820 --> 01:21:07.490 align:middle line:90%
I get back the modulus, right?

01:21:07.490 --> 01:21:11.300 align:middle line:84%
And linearity of the
Riemann-- or not Riemann--

01:21:11.300 --> 01:21:14.420 align:middle line:84%
of the Lesbesguen rule for
complex-valued integrable

01:21:14.420 --> 01:21:15.450 align:middle line:90%
functions still holds.

01:21:15.450 --> 01:21:20.630 align:middle line:90%
So I can pull that alpha inside.

01:21:20.630 --> 01:21:24.150 align:middle line:84%
And now this thing
is equal to this.

01:21:24.150 --> 01:21:25.590 align:middle line:90%
So it's equal to a real number.

01:21:25.590 --> 01:21:26.915 align:middle line:90%
So it's equal to its real part.

01:21:26.915 --> 01:21:33.630 align:middle line:90%


01:21:33.630 --> 01:21:36.480 align:middle line:84%
And now the real
part of the integral

01:21:36.480 --> 01:21:38.310 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the integral
of the real part

01:21:38.310 --> 01:21:40.380 align:middle line:90%
just by the definition.

01:21:40.380 --> 01:21:46.546 align:middle line:84%
So this is equal to the
real part of alpha times f.

01:21:46.546 --> 01:21:50.550 align:middle line:90%


01:21:50.550 --> 01:21:56.800 align:middle line:84%
Now this is a real-valued
function, integrable function.

01:21:56.800 --> 01:22:01.140 align:middle line:84%
And we know from what we proved
for real-valued integrable

01:22:01.140 --> 01:22:06.000 align:middle line:84%
functions, that's less than
or equal to the real part

01:22:06.000 --> 01:22:14.440 align:middle line:84%
of alpha times f over E. Now the
absolute value of the real part

01:22:14.440 --> 01:22:17.680 align:middle line:84%
of a complex number is less
than or equal to the modulus

01:22:17.680 --> 01:22:18.775 align:middle line:90%
of that complex number.

01:22:18.775 --> 01:22:25.880 align:middle line:90%


01:22:25.880 --> 01:22:29.480 align:middle line:84%
And alpha has
modulus equal to 1.

01:22:29.480 --> 01:22:39.120 align:middle line:84%
So this is equal to the
integral of the absolute value.

01:22:39.120 --> 01:22:42.320 align:middle line:84%
So at this point
right here, we used

01:22:42.320 --> 01:22:45.500 align:middle line:84%
what we knew about real-valued
integrable functions.

01:22:45.500 --> 01:22:49.190 align:middle line:84%
And using that, we then got
the corresponding statement

01:22:49.190 --> 01:22:52.940 align:middle line:84%
for complex-valued
integrable functions.

01:22:52.940 --> 01:22:57.440 align:middle line:84%
And using the theorems
that we proved

01:22:57.440 --> 01:22:59.300 align:middle line:84%
for real-valued
integrable functions,

01:22:59.300 --> 01:23:02.780 align:middle line:84%
we get corresponding statements
for complex-valued integrable

01:23:02.780 --> 01:23:05.270 align:middle line:84%
functions as long as the
statements make sense.

01:23:05.270 --> 01:23:09.015 align:middle line:84%
We don't say two
complex-valued functions,

01:23:09.015 --> 01:23:10.640 align:middle line:84%
one is less than or
equal to the other,

01:23:10.640 --> 01:23:12.630 align:middle line:84%
because we don't have an
order on complex numbers.

01:23:12.630 --> 01:23:14.172 align:middle line:84%
So that statement
doesn't make sense.

01:23:14.172 --> 01:23:23.380 align:middle line:84%
But statements
about, for example,

01:23:23.380 --> 01:23:25.740 align:middle line:84%
complex-valued continuous
functions, the Lesbesgue

01:23:25.740 --> 01:23:28.737 align:middle line:84%
integral equaling the
Riemann integral, that

01:23:28.737 --> 01:23:30.570 align:middle line:84%
follows just immediately
because the Riemann

01:23:30.570 --> 01:23:33.360 align:middle line:84%
integral of a complex number--
a complex-valued continuous

01:23:33.360 --> 01:23:36.400 align:middle line:84%
function-- is just defined
to be the same thing.

01:23:36.400 --> 01:23:37.650 align:middle line:90%
And since we know about--

01:23:37.650 --> 01:23:42.420 align:middle line:84%
know that this
equals the Riemann

01:23:42.420 --> 01:23:45.930 align:middle line:84%
integral of the real part of
f, and this equals the Riemann

01:23:45.930 --> 01:23:47.790 align:middle line:84%
integral of the imaginary
part of f, which

01:23:47.790 --> 01:23:52.320 align:middle line:84%
is by definition equal to
the Riemann integral of f,

01:23:52.320 --> 01:23:55.770 align:middle line:84%
then we immediately get the
previous theorem generalized

01:23:55.770 --> 01:24:02.150 align:middle line:90%
to complex-valued functions.

01:24:02.150 --> 01:24:06.402 align:middle line:84%
All right, so that's the
theory of Riemann integration.

01:24:06.402 --> 01:24:07.610 align:middle line:90%
I mean-- Riemann integration?

01:24:07.610 --> 01:24:09.330 align:middle line:90%
Lesbesgue integration.

01:24:09.330 --> 01:24:15.830 align:middle line:84%
Next time, we will finish
our discussion of--

01:24:15.830 --> 01:24:19.590 align:middle line:90%


01:24:19.590 --> 01:24:22.050 align:middle line:84%
measure an integration
by introducing the big Lp

01:24:22.050 --> 01:24:26.610 align:middle line:84%
spaces, which are spaces
of measurable functions

01:24:26.610 --> 01:24:27.675 align:middle line:90%
which have a finite--

01:24:27.675 --> 01:24:30.960 align:middle line:90%


01:24:30.960 --> 01:24:34.290 align:middle line:84%
which raised to a certain power,
have a finite Lesbesguen rule,

01:24:34.290 --> 01:24:40.680 align:middle line:84%
and show that those
are Banach spaces that

01:24:40.680 --> 01:24:43.880 align:middle line:84%
contain the continuous
functions, of course,

01:24:43.880 --> 01:24:46.700 align:middle line:90%
and in certain cases are--

01:24:46.700 --> 01:24:48.800 align:middle line:84%
the space of
